0: 77,420 + 790. 77,420 + 700 = 78,120. Then +90 = 78,210. \n\nBut according to the other method, it's 156,420. \n\nWait, so which is correct? \n\nWait, 790 \u00d7 198. Let me check with another approach. Let's compute 790 \u00d7 198. \n\nAlternatively, 198 can be written as (200 - 2). So 790 \u00d7 198 = 790 \u00d7 (200 - 2) = 790\u00d7200 - 790\u00d72. \n\n790\u00d7200 = 158,000. 790\u00d72 = 1,580. So 158,000 - 1,580 = 156,420. \n\nBut according to the standard multiplication, it's 78,210. \n\nWait, so there's a problem here. Which one is correct? \n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the standard multiplication. Let me check again. \n\nWait, 790 \u00d7 198. Let's do it step by step. \n\nFirst, multiply 790 by 100: 790 \u00d7 100 = 79,000. \n\nThen multiply 790 by 90: 790 \u00d7 90 = 71,100. \n\nThen multiply 790 by 8: 790 \u00d7 8 = 6,320. \n\nNow, adding all three together: 79,000 + 71,100 + 6,320. \n\n79,000 + 71,100 = 150,100. Then 150,100 + 6,320 = 156,420. \n\nAh! There we go. So that's the correct answer. \n\nSo where did I go wrong earlier? \n\nAh! I see. When I did the standard multiplication, I considered the digits as hundreds, tens, units, but I didn't account for the place values properly. Wait, no. Wait, 198 is 1 hundred, 9 tens, 8 units. So when multiplying by 790, which is 7 hundreds, 9 tens, 0 units, the correct way is:\n\nMultiply 790 by 8 (units): 6,320.\n\nMultiply 790 by 90 (tens): 71,100.\n\nMultiply 790 by 100 (hundreds): 79,000.\n\nWait, but 198 is 100 + 90 + 8, so adding those three products: 79,000 + 71,100 + 6,320 = 156,420. \n\nBut earlier, when I did the standard multiplication, I added 6,320 + 71,100 + 790, which was incorrect. Because the hundreds place multiplication should be 790 \u00d7 100 = 79,000, not 790 \u00d7 1. \n\nWait, so my mistake was in the standard multiplication step. I incorrectly multiplied the hundreds digit (1) as 790 \u00d7 1 = 790 instead of 790 \u00d7 100. That's where the error was. \n\nSo the correct way is to