take in one of the steps. Let me check the standard multiplication again.\n\nFirst, 596*3=1728. Correct.\n\nSecond, 596*70. Let's compute 596*70. 596*7=4172. Wait, hold on. 596*7. 6*7=42, write down 2, carry over 4. 9*7=63 +4=67, write down 7, carry over 6. 5*7=35 +6=41, write down 41. So that's 4172. Then multiply by 10: 4172*10=41,720. Wait, earlier I wrote 41,600. That's the mistake! Oh no, I subtracted 400 instead of 400? Wait, no. Wait, 596*70 is 596*7*10. 596*7=4172. Then 4172*10=41,720. But I had written 41,600. That's a mistake. So that's where the error is.\n\nSo in the second step, instead of 41,600, it should be 41,720. Then, adding all three parts: 1728 + 41,720 + 119,200.\n\nFirst, 1728 + 41,720. Let's compute 41,720 + 1,728. 41,720 + 1,000 = 42,720; 42,720 + 700 = 43,420; 43,420 + 28 = 43,448. Then add 119,200. 43,448 + 119,200. 43,448 + 100,000 = 143,448; 143,448 + 19,200 = 162,648. Wait, but that's still different from the first method's 162,708. Hmm, so now there's a new discrepancy.\n\nWait, let me check 596*70 again. 596*70. 596*7=4172. Then 4172*10=41,720. So that's correct. Then why is there a discrepancy?\n\nWait, let's check the original problem again. The question is the product of 596 and 273. So maybe I made a mistake in the standard multiplication steps.\n\nWait, let's do the standard multiplication again, carefully.\n\nMultiplying 596 by 273:\n\nStart with the units digit: 3.\n\n596\n\nx 273\n\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 596 by 3:\n\n3*6=18, write 8, carry 1.\n\n3*9=27 +1=28, write 8, carry 2.\n\n3*5=15 +2=17, write 17.\n\nSo that's 1728.\n\nNext, multiply 596 by 70 (which is 7*10):\n\nStart with the tens digit: 7.\n\n596\n\nx 273\n\n--------\n\n   596*7\n\n--------\n\n   4172\n\nThen, multiply 596 by 200 (which is 2*100):\n\n596\n\nx 273\n\n--------\n\n   596*2\n\n--------\n\n  1192\n\nNow, add them up:\n\n1728 (from 3)\n\n+4172 (from 70)\n\n+1192 (from 200)\n\nLet's add 1728 + 4172 first. 1728 + 4000 = 5728; 5728 + 172 = 5800. So 5728 