      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 216 and 124. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down into smaller parts, maybe using the distributive property. Let me think. 216 is 200 plus 16, right? And 124 is 100 plus 24. So maybe I can multiply each part separately and then add them up. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, multiply 200 by 100. That's straightforward, 200 times 100 is 20,000. Then, 200 times 24. Hmm, 200 times 20 is 4,000 and 200 times 4 is 800, so adding those together gives 4,800. Next, 16 times 100. That's easy, 1600. Then 16 times 24. Oh, 16 times 24... Let me calculate that. 16 times 20 is 320, and 16 times 4 is 64. So 320 plus 64 is 384. \n\nNow, adding all those parts together: 20,000 + 4,800 is 24,800. Then, 1,600 + 384 is 1,984. Finally, adding 24,800 and 1,984. Let's see, 24,800 plus 1,000 is 25,800. Then, 25,800 plus 984. Hmm, 25,800 plus 900 is 26,700, and then plus 84 is 26,784. So, is the product 26,784? Let me check my steps again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nWait, maybe there's a better way to do this multiplication step by step. Let me try another method to verify. Let's use the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\nMultiplying 216 by 124. Let's write it out:\n\n      216\n    x 124\n    ------\n      864   (216 x 4)\n     888    (216 x 20, shifted one position to the left)\n    432     (216 x 100, shifted two positions to the left)\n    ------\n      26784\n\nWait, let me do that again. Starting from the rightmost digit. 4 times 216 is 864. Then, 2 (from 124's tens place) times 216, but we have to shift one position to the left, so it's 888. Then, 1 (from 124's hundreds place) times 216, shifted two positions to the left, which is 432. Adding those up: 864 + 888 + 432. Let's add 864 and 888 first. 800 + 800 is 1,600. 64 + 88 is 152. So 1,600 + 152 is 1,752. Then, add 432 to that. 1,752 + 400 is 2,152. Then, 2,152 + 32 is 2,184. Wait, that's different from the previous 26,784. Hmm, that can't be right. Did I do something wrong here?\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the tens place (2 in 124), you have to shift the partial product one position to the left. So 216 x 2 is 432, but shifted one position becomes 864. Wait, no, hold on. Let me clarify. \n\nActually, when you multiply by the tens place (the second digit from the right in 124), which is 2, you take the partial product and shift it one place to the left. So 216 x 2 is 432, then shift one left to make it 4320. Then, 216 x 4 is 864, no shift needed. Then, 216 x 1 (the hundreds place) is 216, shifted two places to the left, making it 21600. Then add them all up: 864 + 4320 + 21600. Let's compute that. 864 + 4320 is 5184. Then, 5184 + 21600. 5000 + 21600 is 26600, plus 184 is 26784. Oh, okay, so that matches the first method. So the product is 26,784. \n\nSo my initial second method had a mistake in the shifting. I think I confused the positions. So the correct way is to shift each partial product according to the place value. So multiplying by the units digit (4) gives 864, multiplying by the tens digit (2) gives 4320 (shifted one left), multiplying by the hundreds digit (1) gives 21600 (shifted two left). Then adding them up gives 864 + 4320 + 21600 = 26,784. \n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property as I did first: 216 x 124 = 216 x (100 + 20 + 4) = 216*100 + 216*20 + 216*4. Let's compute each term:\n\n216*100 = 21,600\n\n216*20 = 4,320\n\n216*4 = 864\n\nAdding those together: 21,600 + 4,320 = 25,920; 25,920 + 864 = 26,784. Yep, same result. So both methods confirm that the product is 26,784. \n\nJust to be thorough, maybe I can check using another method. Let's use the fact that 216 is 6^3, and 124 is 4*31. So 6^3 * 4*31. 6*4 is 24, so 24*31*31. Wait, 24*31 is 744, then 744*31. Hmm, 744*30 is 22,320 and 744*1 is 744, so total is 22,320 + 744 = 23,064. Wait, that's different. Wait, that can't be. Wait, hold on. Wait, 216 is 6^3, which is 216. 124 is 4*31. So 216*124 = 216*4*31. 216*4 is 864. Then 864*31. Let's compute 864*30 = 25,920 and 864*1 = 864. So 25,920 + 864 = 26,784. Oh, okay, that works. So that's another way to get the same answer. So that confirms it again. \n\nSo all methods lead to 26,784. Therefore, the product of 216 and 124 is 26,784. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations now. So I can be confident that the answer is 26,784.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 216 and 124 is \\boxed{26784}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 216 and 124, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n216 \\times 124 = 216 \\times (100 + 20 + 4)\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down into smaller parts:\n\n1. Calculate \\(216 \\times 100\\):\n   \\[\n   216 \\times 100 = 21600\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(216 \\times 20\\):\n   \\[\n   216 \\times 20 = 4320\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(216 \\times 4\\):\n   \\[\n   216 \\times 4 = 864\n   \\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n21600 + 4320 + 864\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(21600\\) and \\(4320\\):\n\\[\n21600 + 4320 = 25920\n\\]\n\nThen, add the result to \\(864\\):\n\\[\n25920 + 864 = 26784\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  216 \\\\\n\\times 124 \\\\\n\\hline\n  864 \\quad \\text{(216 \\times 4)} \\\\\n 8880 \\quad \\text{(216 \\times 20, shifted one position)} \\\\\n 43200 \\quad \\text{(216 \\times 100, shifted two positions)} \\\\\n\\hline\n 26784 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAdding the partial products:\n\\[\n864 + 8880 + 43200 = 26784\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 216 and 124 is \\(\\boxed{26784}\\).",
