      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 361 and 476. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down, but these numbers are a bit bigger. Maybe I should just go straight for it? Let me think. \n\nFirst, 361 is a three-digit number, and 476 is a three-digit number as well. Multiplying them directly might be a bit tedious, but maybe there's a smarter way. Wait, 361 is actually 19 squared, right? Because 19 times 19 is 361. And 476... Hmm, not sure if that factors into anything. Let me check. 476 divided by 2 is 238, divided by 2 again is 119. 119 is 7 times 17. So 476 is 2 squared times 7 times 17. But I don't know if that helps with multiplying by 361. Maybe not directly. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me try that. \n\nFirst, write down 361 multiplied by 476. Let me set it up like this:\n\n     361\n   x 476\n   -------\n\nStarting with the multiplication of 361 by 6 (the units digit of 476). 361 x 6. Let's compute that. 300 x 6 is 1800, 60 x 6 is 360, 1 x 6 is 6. Adding those together: 1800 + 360 = 2160, plus 6 is 2166. So that's the first partial product: 2166.\n\nNext, multiply 361 by 7 (the tens digit of 476). But wait, in the standard method, you actually multiply by the tens digit and then add a zero. So 361 x 7, then add a zero. Let's do that. 361 x 7: 300 x 7 = 2100, 60 x 7 = 420, 1 x 7 = 7. Adding those: 2100 + 420 = 2520, plus 7 is 2527. Then add a zero, making it 25270.\n\nWait, no, actually, when you multiply by the tens digit, you have to shift the partial product one place to the left. So maybe I should do 361 x 7, which is 2527, and then add a zero to make it 25270. Then the next partial product is 25270.\n\nThen, multiply 361 by 4 (the hundreds digit of 476). Again, similar to before. 361 x 4 is 1444. Since it's the hundreds digit, we add two zeros. So 1444 becomes 144400.\n\nNow, add all three partial products together: 2166 + 25270 + 144400.\n\nLet me add them step by step. First, 2166 + 25270. Let's see, 2166 + 25270. 2000 + 25000 = 27000, 166 + 270 = 436. So total is 27000 + 436 = 27436. Then add 144400 to that. 27436 + 144400. 20000 + 140000 = 160000, 7436 + 4400 = 11836. So total is 160000 + 11836 = 171,836. Wait, is that right? Let me check again.\n\nWait, 2166 + 25270: 2166 + 25270. Let's do 2166 + 25270. 2000 + 25000 = 27000, 166 + 270 = 436. So 27000 + 436 = 27436. Then 27436 + 144400. 27436 + 144400. Let's break it down: 27,436 + 144,400. 27,000 + 144,000 = 171,000. 436 + 400 = 836. So 171,000 + 836 = 171,836. So the total is 171,836. \n\nBut wait, let me verify this with another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using another multiplication technique or checking with a calculator. Wait, since I don't have a calculator, maybe breaking down the numbers differently.\n\nAlternatively, 361 x 476. Let's note that 361 x 400 = 144,400. Then 361 x 70 = 25,270. Then 361 x 6 = 2,166. Adding those together: 144,400 + 25,270 = 169,670. Then 169,670 + 2,166 = 171,836. Okay, same result. So that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using distributive property. Let's see. 361 x 476. Let's express 476 as 400 + 70 + 6. Then:\n\n361 x 400 = 144,400\n\n361 x 70 = 25,270\n\n361 x 6 = 2,166\n\nAdding them up: 144,400 + 25,270 = 169,670; 169,670 + 2,166 = 171,836. Same answer. So that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the fact that 361 is 19^2. Let's see if 19^2 x 476 can be simplified. 19 x 476 x 19. Maybe compute 19 x 476 first, then multiply by 19. Let's try that.\n\n19 x 476. Let's compute 20 x 476 = 9,520. Then subtract 1 x 476 = 476. So 9,520 - 476 = 9,044. Then multiply 9,044 by 19. Hmm, that might be a bit more complicated. Let's do that.\n\n9,044 x 19. Break it down: 9,044 x 10 = 90,440; 9,044 x 9 = 81,396. Then add them together: 90,440 + 81,396. 90,000 + 81,000 = 171,000; 440 + 396 = 836. So total is 171,000 + 836 = 171,836. Same result again. So that's good.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the method, the product is 171,836. So I think that's the answer. Let me just check once more with another approach. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm step by step.\n\nMultiplying 361 by 476:\n\nFirst, multiply 361 by 6: 361 x 6 = 2,166.\n\nThen multiply 361 by 70 (which is 7 x 10): 361 x 7 = 2,527. Then add a zero: 25,270.\n\nThen multiply 361 by 400 (which is 4 x 100): 361 x 4 = 1,444. Add two zeros: 144,400.\n\nNow add all three:\n\n2,166\n\n25,270\n\n144,400\n\nAdding the first two: 2,166 + 25,270 = 27,436.\n\nThen 27,436 + 144,400 = 171,836.\n\nYep, same result. So I think that's correct. Therefore, the product of 361 and 476 is 171,836.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 361 and 476 is \\boxed{171836}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 361 and 476, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 361 by the units digit (6) of 476:\n   \\[\n   361 \\times 6 = 2166\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 361 by the tens digit (7) of 476, then add a zero:\n   \\[\n   361 \\times 7 = 2527 \\quad \\text{Add a zero:} \\quad 25270\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 361 by the hundreds digit (4) of 476, then add two zeros:\n   \\[\n   361 \\times 4 = 1444 \\quad \\text{Add two zeros:} \\quad 144400\n   \\]\n\n4. Add all the partial products together:\n   \\[\n   2166 + 25270 + 144400\n   \\]\n\n   First, add 2166 and 25270:\n   \\[\n   2166 + 25270 = 27436\n   \\]\n\n   Then, add the result to 144400:\n   \\[\n   27436 + 144400 = 171836\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 361 and 476 is \\(\\boxed{171836}\\).",
