61 \u00d7 220. Wait, that's circular. Wait, that's the same as the original problem. So that approach isn't helpful. Wait, maybe I messed up the prime factorization.\n\nWait, 220 is 2 \u00d7 110, and 110 is 10 \u00d7 11. So 220 is 2 \u00d7 10 \u00d7 11, which is 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11, so 2\u00b2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11. So 361 \u00d7 220 = 19\u00b2 \u00d7 2\u00b2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11. So 2\u00b2 \u00d7 2\u00b2 is 2^4, which is 16. Then 5 \u00d7 11 is 55. Then 19\u00b2 is 361. So 16 \u00d7 55 \u00d7 361. Let's compute 16 \u00d7 55 first. 16 \u00d7 50 = 800, 16 \u00d7 5 = 80. So 800 + 80 = 880. Then 880 \u00d7 361. Wait, but 880 \u00d7 361 is 317,680. But that's not the same as 79,420. So where is the mistake here?\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, 361 \u00d7 220. If I factor 220 as 2\u00b2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11, then 361 \u00d7 220 = 361 \u00d7 2\u00b2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11. But 361 is 19\u00b2. So 19\u00b2 \u00d7 2\u00b2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11. So 2\u00b2 \u00d7 2\u00b2 is 16, 5 \u00d7 11 is 55. So 16 \u00d7 55 \u00d7 361. But 16 \u00d7 55 is 880, and 880 \u00d7 361 is 317,680. But that's not the same as 79,420. So clearly, this approach is wrong. Wait, but why?\n\nWait, no. Wait, 361 \u00d7 220. If I factor 220 as 2\u00b2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11, then 361 \u00d7 220 = 361 \u00d7 2\u00b2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11. But 361 is 19\u00b2. So 19\u00b2 \u00d7 2\u00b2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11. So 2\u00b2 \u00d7 2\u00b2 is 16, 5 \u00d7 11 is 55. So 16 \u00d7 55 \u00d7 361. But 16 \u00d7 55 is 880, and 880 \u00d7 361 is 317,680. But that's not the same as 79,420. So this is a contradiction. Therefore, my prime factorization approach must be wrong. Wait, but how?\n\nWait, no. Wait, 361 \u00d7 220. Let's compute 361 \u00d7 220. Let's do it step by step. 361 \u00d7 200 = 72,200. 361 \u00d7 20 = 7,220. Adding them gives 72,200 + 7,220 = 79,420. So that's correct. But when I factor 220 as 2\u00b2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11, then 361 \u00d7 220 = 361 \u00d7 2\u00b2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 11. But 361 is 19\u00b2. So 19\u00b2 \u00d7 