ait, in standard multiplication, when you shift the partial products, the second partial product is shifted one place left, so 854 \u00d7 7 is 5978, but then you write it as 59780? Wait, no. Wait, no, in standard multiplication, each partial product is shifted according to the place value. So the first partial product is units place, so no shift. The second is tens place, so shift one position left (multiply by 10). The third is hundreds place, shift two positions left (multiply by 100). So when you write them out:\n\n      854\n    \u00d7 575\n    ------\n      4270  (854 \u00d7 5)\n    5978    (854 \u00d7 7, shifted one)\n   42700   (854 \u00d7 5, shifted two)\n    ------\n    491050  (sum)\n\nBut when you add them, you have to add the numbers as they are:\n\n4270\n5978\n42700\n--------\n491050\n\nBut 4270 + 5978 + 42700:\n\nFirst, 4270 + 5978:\n\n4270 + 5000 = 9270\n\n9270 + 978 = 10,248\n\nThen 10,248 + 42,700:\n\n10,248 + 40,000 = 50,248\n\n50,248 + 2,700 = 52,948\n\nWait, that's 52,948. But that's not matching the actual total of 491,050. So something is wrong here. Wait, no, actually, no. Wait, 4270 is 4,270. 5978 is 5,978. 42,700 is 42,700. So adding them:\n\n4,270\n\n5,978\n\n+42,700\n\n= (4,270 + 5,978) + 42,700\n\n= 10,248 + 42,700 = 52,948. Wait, but that's not correct. But according to the standard multiplication, the sum should be 491,050. So where is the mistake here?\n\nWait, no, actually, in standard multiplication, the numbers being added are:\n\nFirst partial product: 4270 (which is 854 \u00d7 5, so 4270)\n\nSecond partial product: 5978 (which is 854 \u00d7 7, shifted one position, so 59780? Wait, no. Wait, no, in standard multiplication, when you multiply by the tens digit, you shift the partial product one place to the left. So 854 \u00d7 7 is 5978, but since it's the tens place, you write it as 59780? Wait, no. Wait, no, actually, no. Wait, confusion here. Let me think again.\n\nWait, in standard multiplication, when you multiply by the units digit, you get the first partial product. Then, when you multiply by the tens digit, you take the original multiplicand and multiply by the tens digit, then shift one position to the left (multiply by 10). Similarly, multiplying by the hundreds digit, shift two positions. So in this case:\n\nMultiplicand: 854\n\nMultiply by 5 (units place): 854 \u00d7 5 = 4270 (no shift)\n\nMultiply by 7 (tens place): 854 \u00d7 7 = 5978, then shift one position: 59780\n\nMultiply by 5 (hundreds place): 854 \u00d7 5 = 4270, shift two positions: 427000\n\nThen add them up:\n\n4270\n\n59780\n\n427000\n\n--------\n\n491050\n\nAh! There we go. So I made a mistake earlier by not shifting the partial products properly. So the second partial product should be 5978 shifted one position left, which is 59,780, not 5978. Similarly, the third partial product is 4,270 shifted two positions left, which is 427,000. Then adding them:\n\n4270 + 59,780 = 64,050\n\n64,050 + 427,000 = 491,050\n\nYes, that's correct. So my initial calculation was correct, but when I first tried to do it mentally, I messed up the shifting. So the correct total is 491,050.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's try using the distributive property differently. For example, 854 \u00d7 575 can be written as (800 + 54) \u00d7 (500 + 75). Let's expand that:\n\n800\u00d7500 + 800\u00d775 + 54\u00d7500 + 54\u00d775\n\nCompute each term:\n\n800\u00d7500 = 400,000\n\n800\u00d775 = 60,000\n\n54\u00d7500 = 27,000\n\n54\u00d775 = 3,900\n\nNow add them all together:\n\n400,000 + 60,000 = 460,000\n\n460,000 + 27,000 = 487,000\n\n487,000 + 3,900 = 490,900\n\nWait, that's 490,900. But that's different from 491,050. Hmm, so now there's a discrepancy. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check each term again.\n\n800\u00d7500: 800\u00d7500 is 400,000. Correct.\n\n800\u00d775: 800\u00d775. 800\u00d770=56,000; 800\u00d75=4,000. So 56,000 + 4,000 = 60,000. Correct.\n\n54\u00d7500: 54\u00d7500. 50\u00d7500=25,000; 4\u00