arry over 3,000.\n\nThen, multiply 250 by 900. 250 x 9 is 2,250. Add that 2,250 to the previous 3,000, which gives 5,250. \n\nNow, add up the three results: 2,000 + 3,000 + 5,250. 2,000 + 3,000 is 5,000, plus 5,250 is 10,250. Wait, that doesn't match the previous answers. Hmm, did I do something wrong here?\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check the standard multiplication again. Maybe I messed up the alignment. Let's do it step by step.\n\nMultiplying 250 by 948:\n\nStart with the units place: 8 x 250. 8 x 200 is 1,600, 8 x 50 is 400. So 1,600 + 400 = 2,000. Write down 0, carry over 2,000.\n\nNext, the tens place: 4 x 250. 4 x 200 = 800, 4 x 50 = 200. So 800 + 200 = 1,000. Add that to the previous result. 2,000 + 1,000 = 3,000. Write down 0, carry over 3,000.\n\nNext, the hundreds place: 9 x 250. 9 x 200 = 1,800, 9 x 50 = 450. So 1,800 + 450 = 2,250. Add that to the previous 3,000. 3,000 + 2,250 = 5,250.\n\nNow, add up all the partial products: 2,000 + 3,000 + 5,250. 2,000 + 3,000 is 5,000, plus 5,250 is 10,250. Wait, that's conflicting with the other methods. So now I have three different results: 237,000, 10,250, and 5,250. That can't be right. There must be a mistake here.\n\nWait, no. Wait, in standard multiplication, when you multiply by each digit, you have to consider the place value. So 948 is 9 hundreds, 4 tens, and 8 ones. So when you multiply 250 by 948, you should do:\n\n250 x 8 (ones place) = 2,000\n\n250 x 40 (tens place) = 10,000\n\n250 x 900 (hundreds place) = 225,000\n\nThen add them up: 2,000 + 10,000 + 225,000 = 237,000. Oh! Wait, I think I missed a place value earlier. Let me check again.\n\nIn the standard multiplication:\n\nFirst, multiply 250 by 8: 250 x 8 = 2,000. That's the ones place.\n\nThen, multiply 250 by 40: 250 x 4 = 1,000, so shifted one place to the left (tens place), so 1,000 becomes 10,000.\n\nThen, multiply 250 by 900: 250 x 9 = 2,250, shifted two places to the left (hundreds and thousands), so 2,250 becomes 225,000.\n\nNow, adding them