t's way off. So, which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the standard multiplication. Let me check that again.\n\nOriginal problem: 839 * 132.\n\nStandard multiplication:\n\n      839\n    x 132\n    ------\n      1678  (839 * 2)\n     2517   (839 * 3, shifted one position to the left)\n    839    (839 * 1, shifted two positions to the left)\n    ------\n      110,748\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, in the standard multiplication, the first line is 1678, which is 839 * 2. Then the next line is 2517, which is 839 * 30 (since it's shifted one position to the left). Then the last line is 839, which is 839 * 100 (shifted two positions). Then, adding them up:\n\n1678 + 2517 + 8390. Wait, but in my first calculation, I added 1678 + 2517 + 839, but that was incorrect. Because the last term should be 839 * 100 = 8390, not 839. Oh! There's the mistake. I forgot to shift the last term. So, in the standard multiplication, the third term is 8390, not 839. So, adding 1678 + 2517 + 8390.\n\nLet me recalculate that. 1678 + 2517 is 4195. Then, 4195 + 8390. Let's do 4000 + 8000 = 12,000. 195 + 390 = 585. So, 12,000 + 585 = 12,585. Wait, but that's different from 110,748. Wait, now I'm confused. What's going on here.\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the standard multiplication method, when you multiply by the tens digit (3), you shift the partial product one position to the left, so 839 * 30 becomes 25,170. Then, when you multiply by the hundreds digit (1), you shift it two positions to the left, so 839 * 100 becomes 83,900. Therefore, adding 1678 (which is 839*2) + 25,170 (which is 839*30) + 83,900 (which is 839*100). So, 1678 + 25,170 + 83,900.\n\nLet me compute that again. 1678 + 25,170. 1,678 + 25,170. Let's do 1,000 + 25,000 = 26,000. 678 + 170 = 848. So, 26,000 + 848 = 26,848. Then, add 83,900. 26,848 + 83,900. 20,000 + 80,000 = 100,000. 6,848 + 3,900 = 10,748. Then, 100,000 + 10,748 = 110,748. Ah! There we go. So, the correct sum is 110,748. So, my initial mistake was forgetting to shif