e partial products properly. Each time we multiply by a digit in the multiplier (292), we shift the partial product one more place to the left. So:\n\n- Units digit (2): no shift, 1,830\n\n- Tens digit (9): shift one left, 8,2350 (which is 82,350)\n\n- Hundreds digit (2): shift two left, 18,3000 (which is 183,000)\n\nThen adding them: 1,830 + 82,350 + 183,000 = 267,180. That's correct.\n\nSo my initial error was not shifting the partial products properly. Therefore, the correct answer is 267,180.\n\nBut wait, earlier when I thought I did the first method, I incorrectly wrote 915 x 200 as 18,300 instead of 183,000. That was the mistake. So the correct way is to recognize that each partial product must be shifted appropriately, leading to 267,180.\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property:\n\n915 x 292 = 915 x (300 - 8) = 915 x 300 - 915 x 8 = 274,500 - 7,320 = 267,180. Which matches the correct result.\n\nTherefore, the product of 915 and 292 is 267,180.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 915 and 292 is \\boxed{267180}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 915 and 292, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 292 into 200 + 90 + 2:\n\n1. Calculate \\( 915 \\times 200 \\):\n   \\[\n   915 \\times 200 = 183000\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\( 915 \\times 90 \\):\n   \\[\n   915 \\times 90 = 82350\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\( 915 \\times 2 \\):\n   \\[\n   915 \\times 2 = 1830\n   \\]\n\n4. Add all the partial products together:\n   \\[\n   183000 + 82350 + 1830 = 267180\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 915 and 292 is \\(\\boxed{267180}\\).",
