      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 271 and 797. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two three-digit numbers can be done step by step, but maybe there's a shortcut or a trick to make it easier. Let me think.\n\nFirst, maybe I can break down 271 into 200 + 70 + 1. Then multiply each part by 797 and add them up. That might work. Let's try that.\n\nSo, 271 is 200 + 70 + 1. Therefore:\n\n271 \u00d7 797 = (200 + 70 + 1) \u00d7 797\n\nWhich is the same as:\n\n200\u00d7797 + 70\u00d7797 + 1\u00d7797\n\nLet me compute each part separately.\n\nStarting with 200\u00d7797. Well, 200 is 2\u00d7100, so 2\u00d7797 is 1594, then add the two zeros: 15940. So that part is 15,940.\n\nNext, 70\u00d7797. Similarly, 70 is 7\u00d710, so 7\u00d7797. Let me compute 7\u00d7700 first, which is 4,900. Then 7\u00d797. Hmm, 7\u00d790 is 630, and 7\u00d77 is 49, so 630 + 49 = 679. So 4,900 + 679 = 5,579. Then add the two zeros from the 70: 55,790. Wait, no. Wait, 70\u00d7797 is 7\u00d7797\u00d710. So 7\u00d7797 is 5,579, so 5,579\u00d710 is 55,790. Yeah, that's right.\n\nThen the last part is 1\u00d7797, which is just 797.\n\nNow add all three results together: 15,940 + 55,790 + 797.\n\nLet me add 15,940 and 55,790 first. 15,940 + 55,790. Let's see, 15,000 + 55,000 is 70,000. Then 940 + 790 is 1,730. So total is 70,000 + 1,730 = 71,730. Then add 797 to that. 71,730 + 797. Let's break it down: 71,730 + 700 = 72,430. Then 72,430 + 97 = 72,527. So the total is 72,527.\n\nWait, let me check that again. Maybe I made a mistake in adding.\n\nFirst, 15,940 + 55,790. Let's do it digit by digit:\n\n15,940\n\n+55,790\n\n=?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\n4 + 9 = 13, carry over 1\n\n9 + 7 + 1 = 17, carry over 1\n\n5 + 5 + 1 = 11, carry over 1\n\n1 + 5 + 1 = 7\n\nSo that's 71,730. Yeah, that's correct.\n\nThen adding 797:\n\n71,730\n\n+   797\n\n=?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 + 7 = 7\n\n3 + 9 = 12, carry over 1\n\n7 + 7 + 1 = 15, carry over 1\n\n1 + 0 + 1 =