7; 26,287 +189,700 = 216,987.\n\nBut these two results are different. That can't be. So one of the methods is wrong. Which one?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the second method. Let me check each step again.\n\nFirst, 271\u00d77. 200\u00d77=1,400; 70\u00d77=490; 1\u00d77=7. 1,400+490=1,890; 1,890+7=1,897. That's correct.\n\nThen 271\u00d790. 271\u00d79=2,439; 2,439\u00d710=24,390. Correct.\n\nThen 271\u00d7700. 271\u00d77=1,897; 1,897\u00d7100=189,700. Correct.\n\nAdding them: 1,897 +24,390. Let's compute 1,897 +24,390. 1,000 +24,000=25,000. 897 +390=1,287. So 25,000 +1,287=26,287. Then 26,287 +189,700. 26,287 +189,700. 26,000 +189,700=215,700. 287 +0=287. So 215,700 +287=216,987. Hmm, so according to this, the product is 216,987. But according to the first method, it's 72,527. These are vastly different. So there's a problem here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check that again.\n\nFirst method: 271\u00d7797 = (200 +70 +1)\u00d7797 = 200\u00d7797 +70\u00d7797 +1\u00d7797.\n\n200\u00d7797: 200\u00d7700=140,000; 200\u00d797=19,400. So 140,000 +19,400=159,400. Wait, that's different from what I had before. Wait, earlier I thought 200\u00d7797 is 15,940. Wait, that's wrong. Wait, 200\u00d7797. 200\u00d7700=140,000; 200\u00d797=19,400. So 140,000 +19,400=159,400. But in my first calculation, I said 200\u00d7797=15,940. That's incorrect. Oh no! That's a big mistake. So that's where the error is.\n\nWait, so in the first method, I miscalculated 200\u00d7797. That's a critical error. So let's recalculate.\n\nFirst, 200\u00d7797. 200\u00d7700=140,000; 200\u00d797=19,400. So 140,000 +19,400=159,400. Then 70\u00d7797. Let's compute 70\u00d7700=49,000; 70\u00d797=6,790. So 49,000 +6,790=55,790. Then 1\u00d7797=797. Now add them all together: 159,400 +55,790 +797.\n\nFirst, 159,400 +55,790. Let's compute:\n\n159,400\n\n+55,790\n\n=?\n\n159,400 +50,000=209,400\n\n209,400 +5,790=215,190\n\nThen add 797: 215,190 +797.\n\n215,190 +700=215,890\n\n215,890 +97=215,987.\n\nWait, so the correct total is 215,987. But earlier, when I did the first method, I had 72,527, which was wrong because I miscalculated 200\u00d7797 as 15,940 instead of 159,400. That was a mistake in the first step. So the correct answer is 215,987.\n\nBut then why did the second method give 216,987? Wait, let's check the second method again.\n\nWait, in the second method, when I did 271\u00d77=1,897; 271\u00d790=24,390; 271\u00d7700=189,700. Then adding them: 1,897 +24,390=26,287; 26,287 +189,700=216,987. But according to the first method, the correct answer is 215,987. So there's a discrepancy of 1,000. Hmm. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the second method. Let me check each multiplication again.\n\nFirst, 271\u00d77. 200\u00d77=1,400; 70\u00d77=490; 1\u00d77=7. 1,400+490=1,890; 1,890+7=1,897. Correct.\n\n271\u00d790. 271\u00d79=2,439; 2,439\u00d710=24,390. Correct.\n\n271\u00d7700. 271\u00d77=1,897; 1,897\u00d7100=189,700. Correct.\n\nAdding them: 1,897 +24,390=26,287. Then 26,287 +189,700=216,987. But according to the first method, the correct answer is 215,987. So there's a difference of 1,000. Where is the mistake?\n\nWait, perhaps in the first method, when I broke down 271 into 200 +70 +1, but 200\u00d7797 is 159,400, 70\u00d7797 is 55,790, and 1\u00d7797 is 797. Then adding 159,400 +55,790=215,190 +797=215,987. So that's correct.\n\nBut in the second method, the total was 216,987. So why the difference? Let me check the second method again.\n\nWait, 271\u00d77=1,897. Then 271\u00d790=24,390. Then 271\u00d7700=189,700. Then adding them: 1,897 +24,390=26,287. Then 26,287 +189,700=216,987. But according to the first method, it's 215,987. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check 200\u00d7797 again. 200\u00d7797. 200\u00d7700=140,000; 200\u00d797=19,400. So 140,000 +19,400=159,400. Then 70\u00d7797. 70\u00d7700=49,000; 70\u00d797=6,790. So 49,000 +6,790=55,790. Then 1\u00d7797=797. So total is 159,400 +55,790=215,190 +797=215,987. Correct.\n\nBut in the second method, the total is 216,987. So there's a discrepancy of 1,000. Wait, maybe I added wrong in the second method. Let me check:\n\nFirst, 271\u00d77=1,897. Then 271\u00d790=24,390. Then 271\u00d7700=189,700. Then adding them:\n\n1,897 +24,390. Let's compute 1,897 +24,390. 1,000 +24,000=25,000. 897 +390=1,287. So 25,000 +1,287=26,287. Then 26,287 +189,700. 26,287 +189,700. 26,000 +189,700=215,700. Then 287 +0=287. So 215,700 +287=215,987. Wait, hold on! Wait, 26,287 +189,700. Let's compute 26,287 +189,700. 26,287 +189,700. 26,287 +180,000=206,287. Then 206,287 +9,700=215,987. Oh! Wait, I think I messed up the commas. 189,700 is 180,000 +9,700. So 26,287 +180,000=206,287. Then 206,287 +9,700=215,987. So the second method actually gives 215,987. So my previous calculation had a mistake in adding 26,287 +189,700 as 216,987, but actually, it's 215,987. So both methods give the same result. Phew! So the correct answer is 215,987.\n\nSo where did I go wrong initially in the second method? When I thought 26,287 +189,700 was 216,987, but actually, 26,287 +189,700 is 215,987. So the comma was misplaced. So both methods confirm that the product is 215,987.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 215,987.\n\nBut let me verify with another method to be absolutely sure. Let's use the distributive property differently.\n\nAlternatively, note that 797 is 800 - 3. So 271\u00d7797 = 271\u00d7(800 -3) = 271\u00d7800 -271\u00d73.\n\nCompute 271\u00d7800. 271\u00d78=2,168; then add two zeros: 216,800.\n\nThen 271\u00d73=813.\n\nSubtract: 216,800 -813. 216,800 -800=216,000. Then subtract 13: 216,000 -13=215,987. There we go. So that's another way to compute it, and it gives 215,987. So that's consistent with the first two methods. Therefore, the correct answer is 215,987.\n\nSo the initial mistake in the first method was a miscalculation of 200\u00d7797. Once that was corrected, all methods gave the same result. Therefore, the product of 271 and 797 is 215,987.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 271 and 797 is \\boxed{215987}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 271 and 797, we can use the distributive property:\n\nFirst, break down 271 into 200 + 70 + 1. Then multiply each part by 797 and add the results:\n\n\\[\n271 \\times 797 = (200 + 70 + 1) \\times 797\n\\]\n\nCalculate each part separately:\n\n1. \\(200 \\times 797\\):\n   \\[\n   200 \\times 797 = 200 \\times (800 - 3) = 200 \\times 800 - 200 \\times 3 = 160000 - 600 = 159400\n   \\]\n\n2. \\(70 \\times 797\\):\n   \\[\n   70 \\times 797 = 7 \\times 797 \\times 10 = (7 \\times 700 + 7 \\times 97) \\times 10 = (4900 + 679) \\times 10 = 5579 \\times 10 = 55790\n   \\]\n\n3. \\(1 \\times 797\\):\n   \\[\n   1 \\times 797 = 797\n   \\]\n\nAdd all the results together:\n\n\\[\n159400 + 55790 + 797\n\\]\n\nFirst, add 159400 and 55790:\n\n\\[\n159400 + 55790 = 215190\n\\]\n\nThen add 797:\n\n\\[\n215190 + 797 = 215987\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 271 and 797 is \\(\\boxed{215987}\\).",
