      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 140 and 127. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. Let me think. \n\nFirst, 140 is the same as 100 plus 40, right? So, if I split 140 into 100 and 40, then multiply each part by 127 and add the results together, that should work. Let me write that out:\n\n140 \u00d7 127 = (100 + 40) \u00d7 127\n\nNow, applying the distributive property, which is (a + b) \u00d7 c = a\u00d7c + b\u00d7c, so:\n\n= 100 \u00d7 127 + 40 \u00d7 127\n\nAlright, let's compute each part separately. \n\nStarting with 100 \u00d7 127. Well, multiplying by 100 is straightforward; you just add two zeros. So 100 \u00d7 127 is 12,700. That part seems easy.\n\nNext, 40 \u00d7 127. Hmm, 40 is 4 times 10, so maybe I can do 4 \u00d7 127 first and then multiply by 10. Let me try that. \n\n4 \u00d7 127. Let's break 127 into 100 + 20 + 7. So:\n\n4 \u00d7 100 = 400\n\n4 \u00d7 20 = 80\n\n4 \u00d7 7 = 28\n\nAdding those together: 400 + 80 = 480; 480 + 28 = 508. So, 4 \u00d7 127 is 508. Then, multiplying by 10 (since 40 is 4\u00d710) gives 508 \u00d7 10 = 5,080.\n\nNow, add the two results together: 12,700 + 5,080. Let's see. 12,700 + 5,000 is 17,700. Then, adding the remaining 80 gives 17,780. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nFirst part: 100 \u00d7 127 = 12,700. Correct. \n\nSecond part: 40 \u00d7 127. Breaking down 40 into 4 \u00d7 10. 4 \u00d7 127 = 508. Then 508 \u00d7 10 = 5,080. That seems right. \n\nAdding 12,700 and 5,080. Let's do it step by step. 12,700 + 5,000 = 17,700. Then 17,700 + 80 = 17,780. Yes, that adds up. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method to verify. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm. \n\nMultiplying 140 by 127. Let's write it out:\n\n      140\n    \u00d7 127\n    ------\n      140 \u00d7 7 = 980\n     140 \u00d7 20 = 2,800\n    140 \u00d7 100 = 14,000\n    ------\n    Adding them up: 980 + 2,800 = 3,780; 3,780 + 14,000 = 17,780. \n\nSame result! Okay, that's reassuring. So both methods give me 17,780. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using another decomposition. Let's see, 127 is 130 - 3. So, 140 \u00d7 (130 - 3) = 140 \u00d7 130 - 140 \u00d7 3. \n\n140 \u00d7 130. 140 \u00d7 100 = 14,000; 140 \u00d7 30 = 4,200. So 14,000 + 4,200 = 18,200. Then subtract 140 \u00d7 3 = 420. So 18,200 - 420 = 17,780. Again, same answer. \n\nHmm, seems like 17,780 is the consistent result. I think that's the correct answer. Let me just verify once more with a calculator-like multiplication. \n\nLet's do 140 \u00d7 127. \n\nFirst, multiply 140 by 7: 140 \u00d7 7. 100 \u00d77=700, 40\u00d77=280. 700+280=980. \n\nThen, multiply 140 by 20 (since 2 in the tens place): 140 \u00d7 20. 140 \u00d7 2 = 280, so \u00d720 is 2,800. \n\nThen, multiply 140 by 100 (since 1 in the hundreds place): 140 \u00d7 100 = 14,000. \n\nNow, add them all together: 980 + 2,800 = 3,780. Then 3,780 + 14,000 = 17,780. \n\nYep, same result. So all methods lead to 17,780. I think that's solid. \n\nJust to be thorough, maybe I can check with another approach. For example, using the fact that 140 is 14 \u00d7 10. So 14 \u00d7 10 \u00d7 127. \n\nFirst, 14 \u00d7 127. Let's compute that. 14 \u00d7 100 = 1,400; 14 \u00d7 20 = 280; 14 \u00d7 7 = 98. Adding those: 1,400 + 280 = 1,680; 1,680 + 98 = 1,778. Then multiply by 10: 1,778 \u00d7 10 = 17,780. \n\nSame answer again. Okay, so no matter how I break it down, the product is 17,780. \n\nI think that's pretty conclusive. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and multiple methods confirm the same result. So I'm confident that the product of 140 and 127 is 17,780.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 140 and 127 is \\boxed{17780}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 140 and 127, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n140 \\times 127 = (100 + 40) \\times 127\n\\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n= 100 \\times 127 + 40 \\times 127\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n\n1. \\(100 \\times 127 = 12,700\\)\n2. \\(40 \\times 127\\):\n   - Break down 40 into \\(4 \\times 10\\)\n   - \\(4 \\times 127 = 508\\)\n   - Then multiply by 10: \\(508 \\times 10 = 5,080\\)\n\nAdding the results together:\n\n\\[\n12,700 + 5,080 = 17,780\n\\]\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n 140 \\\\\n\\times 127 \\\\\n\\hline\n 980 \\quad (\\text{140} \\times 7) \\\\\n5,080 \\quad (\\text{140} \\times 20) \\\\\n14,000 \\quad (\\text{140} \\times 100) \\\\\n\\hline\n17,780 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nVerification using another method (140 as 14 \u00d7 10):\n\n\\[\n14 \\times 127 = 1,778 \\\\\n1,778 \\times 10 = 17,780\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{17780}\\).",
