      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 927 and 540. Hmm, let's see. Multiplying two three-digit numbers. Alright, maybe I can break this down step by step. Let me recall how multiplication works. \n\nFirst, I can think of 927 as 900 + 27, right? So, 927 is 900 plus 27. Then, 540 is 500 + 40. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. That is, (a + b)(c + d) = a*c + a*d + b*c + b*d. Let me try that.\n\nSo, if I let a = 900, b = 27, c = 500, d = 40. Then, according to the distributive property:\n\n900*500 + 900*40 + 27*500 + 27*40.\n\nLet me compute each part separately.\n\nFirst term: 900*500. Well, 900 times 500. 900 is 9*100, and 500 is 5*100. So, multiplying them would be (9*5)*(100*100) = 45*10,000 = 450,000. So that's 450,000.\n\nSecond term: 900*40. Hmm, 900*40. 900 is 9*100, so 9*40*100. 9*40 is 360, so 360*100 = 36,000. So that's 36,000.\n\nThird term: 27*500. 27*500. 27 is 27, so 27*500. 27*5 is 135, so 135*100 = 13,500. So that's 13,500.\n\nFourth term: 27*40. 27*40. 27*4 is 108, so 108*10 = 1,080. So that's 1,080.\n\nNow, adding all these four terms together: 450,000 + 36,000 + 13,500 + 1,080.\n\nLet me add them step by step. First, 450,000 + 36,000. That's 486,000. Then, adding 13,500 to that. 486,000 + 13,500 = 499,500. Then, adding 1,080. 499,500 + 1,080 = 500,580.\n\nWait, so the total is 500,580? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nFirst term: 900*500 = 450,000. Correct. Second term: 900*40 = 36,000. Correct. Third term: 27*500 = 13,500. Correct. Fourth term: 27*40 = 1,080. Correct. Adding them up: 450,000 + 36,000 is 486,000. Then 486,000 + 13,500 is 499,500. Then 499,500 + 1,080 is 500,580. Hmm, seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm to verify.\n\nMultiplying 927 by 540. Let's set it up vertically:\n\n    927\n  x 540\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 927 by 0 (the units place of 540). That gives 0.\n\nThen, multiply 9