 from before. Wait, no, wait. Wait, 841 x 700 is 588,700. Then 841 x 60 is 50,460. Then 841 x 4 is 3,364. So adding 588,700 + 50,460 first: 588,700 + 50,000 = 638,700; 638,700 + 460 = 639,160. Then add 3,364: 639,160 + 3,000 = 642,160; 642,160 + 364 = 642,524. Wait, but earlier I got 112,694. That's a discrepancy. Wait, that can't be. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, 841 x 764. Wait, 764 is 700 + 60 + 4. So 841 x 700 is 588,700. Then 841 x 60 is 50,460. Then 841 x 4 is 3,364. So adding those three: 588,700 + 50,460 + 3,364. Let's compute 588,700 + 50,460 first. 588,700 + 50,000 = 638,700. Then 638,700 + 460 = 639,160. Then 639,160 + 3,364. 639,160 + 3,000 = 642,160. Then 642,160 + 364 = 642,524. Wait, but that's 642,524. But earlier, when I did the standard multiplication, I got 112,694. That's way off. Wait, so which one is correct?\n\nWait, hold on. There's a mistake here. Wait, 841 x 764. Wait, 764 is 700 + 60 + 4. So 841 x 700 is 841*7*100. 841*7: 800*7=5600, 40*7=280, 1*7=7. So 5600+280=5880+7=5887. Then 5887*100=588,700. Then 841*60: 841*6*10. 841*6=5046. 5046*10=50,460. Then 841*4=3,364. So adding 588,700 + 50,460 + 3,364. Let's compute 588,700 + 50,460 first. 588,700 + 50,000 = 638,700. Then 638,700 + 460 = 639,160. Then 639,160 + 3,364. 639,160 + 3,000 = 642,160. Then 642,160 + 364 = 642,524. So according to this, the product is 642,524. But earlier, when I did the standard multiplication step by step, I got 112,694. That's a problem. There must be a mistake here. Wait, why the discrepancy?\n\nWait, no, hold on. Wait, 841 x 764. Wait, 764 is 700 + 60 + 4. So 841 x 700 is 588,700. Then 841 x 60 is 50,460. Then 841 x 4 is 3,364. So adding those three numbers: 588,700 + 50,460 + 3,364. Let's compute 588,700 + 50,460 first. 588,700 + 50,000 = 638,700. Then 638,700 + 460 = 639,160. Then 639,160 + 3,364. 639,160 + 3,000 = 642,160. Then 642,160 + 364 = 642,524. So that's 642,524. But when I did the standard multiplication earlier, I got 112,694. That's way different. So which one is correct?\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me check with another method. Let's compute 841 x 764 using another approach. Let's use the standard multiplication algorithm again.\n\n      841\n    x 764\n    ------\n      841   (841 x 4)\n     8410   (841 x 60, shifted one)\n    6843    (841 x 700, shifted two)\n    ------\n     642,524\n\nWait, so the three partial products are 3,364; 50,460; and 68,430. Wait, hold on, 841 x 700 is 58,870. Wait, but in the standard multiplication, the third partial product is 68,430. Wait, that's different. Wait, maybe I made a mistake in the third partial product. Let me check.\n\nWait, 841 x 700. 800 x 700 = 560,000. 40 x 700 = 28,000. 1 x 700 = 700. So 560,000 + 28,000 = 588,000. 588,000 + 700 = 588,700. So that's correct. But in the standard multiplication, the third partial product is 68,430. Wait, that's not matching. Wait, why is there a discrepancy?\n\nWait, no. Wait, in the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the hundreds digit, you shift the partial product two places to the left. So 841 x 700 is 588,700. But in the standard multiplication layout, the third partial product is written as 68,430. Wait, that's not matching. Wait, maybe I messed up the alignment.\n\nWait, let me write it out properly:\n\n        841\n      x 764\n      ------\n        841   (841 x 4)\n       8410   (841 x 60, shifted one)\n      6843    (841 x 700, shifted two)\n      ------\n      642,524\n\nWait, but 841 x 700 is 588,700. So the third partial product should be 588,700 shifted two places left, which is 588,700 x 100 = 58,870,000. But that's not what's written here. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, in the standard multiplication, when you multiply by the hundreds digit, you shift the partial product two places to the left. So 841 x 700 is 588,700. But when written in the multiplication layout, it's written as 68,430. Wait, that's not matching. Wait, maybe I made a mistake in the multiplication.\n\nWait, 841 x 700. Let's compute 841 x