ch one is correct?\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me check again. Wait, if 220 is 200 + 20, then 220 \u00d7 682 = 200\u00d7682 + 20\u00d7682. \n\n200\u00d7682: 200\u00d7600=120,000; 200\u00d780=16,000; 200\u00d72=400. So 120,000 + 16,000 = 136,000; 136,000 + 400 = 136,400. That's correct.\n\n20\u00d7682: 20\u00d7600=12,000; 20\u00d780=1,600; 20\u00d72=40. So 12,000 + 1,600 = 13,600; 13,600 + 40 = 13,640. Correct.\n\nAdding 136,400 + 13,640. Let's do this step by step:\n\n136,400 + 10,000 = 146,400\n\n146,400 + 3,000 = 149,400\n\n149,400 + 640 = 149,400 + 600 = 149,000; 149,000 + 40 = 149,040.\n\nWait, so according to this, the total is 149,040. But earlier methods gave 150,040. There's a conflict here. Which one is wrong?\n\nWait, this is concerning. Let me check where the error is. Let's recalculate 220 \u00d7 682 using the standard multiplication method again.\n\nMultiplying 220 by 682:\n\nStart with the units digit: 2 \u00d7 220 = 440. Write down 440.\n\nNext, the tens digit: 8 \u00d7 220 = 1,760. But since it's the tens place, we need to shift one position to the left: 1,760 becomes 17,600.\n\nNext, the hundreds digit: 6 \u00d7 220 = 1,320. Shift two positions: 132,000.\n\nNow, add them up:\n\n440\n\n17,600\n\n132,000\n\nAdding 440 + 17,600: 440 + 17,600 = 18,040.\n\n18,040 + 132,000 = 150,040.\n\nWait, so according to this, the total is 150,040. But when I broke down 220 into 200 + 20, I got 149,040. There's a discrepancy of 1,000. That's a problem. Where is the mistake?\n\nWait, let me check the breakdown again. 220 is 200 + 20. So 200 \u00d7 682 + 20 \u00d7 682.\n\n200 \u00d7 682: 200 \u00d7 600 = 120,000; 200 \u00d7 80 = 16,000; 200 \u00d7 2 = 400. So 120,000 + 16,000 = 136,000; 136,000 + 400 = 136,400. That's correct.\n\n20 \u00d7 682: 20 \u00d7 600 = 12,000; 20 \u00d7 80 = 1,600; 20 \u00d7 2 = 40. So 12,000 + 1,600 = 13,600; 13,600 + 40 = 13,640. Correct.\n\n136,400 + 13,640. Let's add these two numbers:\n\n136,400\n\n+ 13,640\n\n= ?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\n0 + 4 = 4\n\n4 + 6 = 10, carry over 1\n\n6 + 3 + 1 (carry) = 10, carry over 1\n\n3 + 1 + 1 (carry) = 5\n\n1 + 0 = 1\n\nWait, that can't be. Wait, let's write them aligned:\n\n136,400\n\n+ 13,640\n\n= ?\n\n136,400\n\n+ 13,640\n\n= 149,040.\n\nWait, but when I added 136,400 + 13,640, I thought it was 149,040. But according to the standard multiplication, it's 150,040. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in adding. Let's do 136,400 + 13,640.\n\n136,400 + 10,000 = 146,400\n\n146,400 + 3,000 = 149,400\n\n149,400 + 640 = 149,400 + 640 = 150,040.\n\nAh! There we go. So I must have miscalculated earlier. So 136,400 + 13,640 is 150,040. So my initial addition was correct. I must have added 136,400 + 13,640 as 149,040 due to a miscalculation. Let me check again:\n\n136,400 + 13,640:\n\nStart with the thousands:\n\n136,400 has 136 thousand and 400.\n\n13,640 has 13 thousand and 640.\n\nAdding the thousands: 136,000 + 13,000 = 149,000.\n\nAdding the remaining 400 + 640 = 1,040.\n\nSo 149,000 + 1,040 = 150,040. There we go. So the correct sum is 150,040. So my initial breakdown was correct. The mistake was in the intermediate step where I thought it was 149,040, but that was an error in adding. So all methods confirm that the product is 150,040.\n\nTherefore, I can be confident that the product of 220 and 682 is 150,040.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 220 and 682 is \\boxed{150040}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 220 and 682, we can break down the multiplication into simpler steps:\n\n1. **Decompose 682 into 600, 80, and 2:**\n   \\[\n   220 \\times 682 = 220 \\times (600 + 80 + 2)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 220 \\times 600 + 220 \\times 80 + 220 \\times 2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate each part separately:**\n   \\[\n   220 \\times 600 = 132000\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   220 \\times 80 = 17600\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   220 \\times 2 = 440\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sum the results:**\n   \\[\n   132000 + 17600 + 440\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 132000 + 17600 = 149600\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   149600 + 440 = 150040\n   \\]\n\n4. **Verification using standard multiplication method:**\n   - Multiply 220 by 2 (units place of 682): \\(220 \\times 2 = 440\\)\n   - Multiply 220 by 8 (tens place of 682): \\(220 \\times 8 = 1760\\) (shifted one position: 17600)\n   - Multiply 220 by 6 (hundreds place of 682): \\(220 \\times 6 = 1320\\) (shifted two positions: 132000)\n   - Sum the results: \\(440 + 17600 + 132000 = 150040\\)\n\n5. **Verification by breaking down 220 into 200 and 20:**\n   - \\(200 \\times 682 = 136400\\)\n   - \\(20 \\times 682 = 13640\\)\n   - Sum the results: \\(136400 + 13640 = 150040\\)\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 220 and 682 is \\(\\boxed{150040}\\).",
