      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 114 and 872. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done step by step, breaking them down if it's easier. Let me think. Maybe I can use the standard multiplication method? Let's try that.\n\nFirst, let me write down 114 and 872 one on top of the other, aligning the digits:\n\n   114\n\u00d7 872\n-------\n\nNow, I need to multiply 114 by 800, then 114 by 70, and then 114 by 2, right? Because 872 is 800 + 70 + 2. Let me check that. Yes, 800 + 70 is 870, plus 2 is 872. So that works.\n\nStarting with 114 \u00d7 800. Hmm, multiplying by 800. Well, 114 \u00d7 8 is 912, and then add two zeros because it's 800. So 912 followed by two zeros is 912,000. Wait, is that right? Let me verify. 114 \u00d7 8 = 912, yes. Then 912 \u00d7 100 is 91,200, and 91,200 \u00d7 100 is 9,120,000. Wait, no, that's not. Wait, no, 800 is 8 \u00d7 100. So 114 \u00d7 800 is (114 \u00d7 8) \u00d7 100. So 114 \u00d7 8 is 912, then 912 \u00d7 100 is 91,200. Oh, I made a mistake earlier. So 114 \u00d7 800 is 91,200. Got it.\n\nNext, 114 \u00d7 70. Hmm, 114 \u00d7 7 is 800, right? Because 100 \u00d7 7 is 700, and 14 \u00d7 7 is 98, so 700 + 98 = 798. Wait, no, 114 \u00d7 7. Let me compute that again. 100 \u00d7 7 = 700, 10 \u00d7 7 = 70, 4 \u00d7 7 = 28. So 700 + 70 + 28 = 798. So 114 \u00d7 70 is 798 \u00d7 10 = 7,980. Yes, that's correct.\n\nThen, 114 \u00d7 2. That's straightforward. 114 \u00d7 2 = 228.\n\nNow, add all those three results together: 91,200 + 7,980 + 228.\n\nLet me add 91,200 and 7,980 first. 91,200 + 7,000 is 98,200. Then add 980: 98,200 + 980 = 99,180. Then add 228: 99,180 + 228 = 99,408.\n\nWait, so the total is 99,408? Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nFirst step: 114 \u00d7 800 = 91,200. Correct.\n\nSecond step: 114 \u00d7 70 = 7,980. Correct.\n\nThird step: 114 \u00d7 2 = 228. Correct.\n\nAdding them up: 91,200 + 7,980. Let's do 91,200 + 7,000 = 98,200. Then 98,200 + 980. 98,200 + 900 = 99,100. Then 99,100 + 80 = 99,180. Then 99,180 + 228. 99,180 + 200 = 99,380. Then 99,380 + 28 = 99,408. Yes, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm step by step.\n\nMultiplying 114 by 872:\n\nFirst, multiply 114 by 2 (units place):\n\n114\n\u00d7   2\n------\n228\n\nThen multiply 114 by 7 (tens place), but since it's 70, we shift one position to the left:\n\n114\n\u00d7  7\n------\n798\n     (shifted left by one)\n------\n7,980\n\nThen multiply 114 by 8 (hundreds place), shifted two positions to the left:\n\n114\n\u00d7  8\n------\n912\n    (shifted left by two)\n------\n91,200\n\nNow, add them up:\n\n   91,200\n+  7,980\n+    228\n--------\n99,408\n\nYes, same result. So that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the distributive property differently. Let's see. 872 can be written as 800 + 70 + 2, which we already did. Alternatively, maybe break down 114 into 100 + 14. Let's try that.\n\n114 = 100 + 14. So 114 \u00d7 872 = (100 + 14) \u00d7 872 = 100\u00d7872 + 14\u00d7872.\n\nCompute 100\u00d7872 = 87,200.\n\nCompute 14\u00d7872. Let's do 10\u00d7872 = 8,720 and 4\u00d7872 = 3,488. Then add them: 8,720 + 3,488 = 12,208.\n\nNow, add 87,200 + 12,208. 87,200 + 12,000 = 99,200. Then add 208: 99,200 + 208 = 99,408. Same answer. So that's good.\n\nAnother way: Maybe use the calculator-like multiplication. Let's do it step by step.\n\nMultiplying 114 \u00d7 872:\n\nStart with the units digit of 872, which is 2. Multiply 114 \u00d7 2 = 228. Write down 228.\n\nNext digit is 7 (tens place). Multiply 114 \u00d7 7 = 800. But since it's the tens place, we write 0 in the tens place. So 800 becomes 8000.\n\nNext digit is 8 (hundreds place). Multiply 114 \u00d7 8 = 912. Write that down. So now, combining all:\n\n912 (hundreds place)\n+ 8000 (tens place)\n+ 228 (units place)\n= 912 + 8000 = 8912; 8912 + 228 = 9140? Wait, that doesn't match. Wait, maybe I messed up the