on. For example, 114 = 100 + 14, as before. So 100\u00d7872 = 87,200 and 14\u00d7872 = 12,208. Adding those gives 87,200 + 12,208 = 99,408. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, use the fact that 872 \u00d7 100 = 87,200, 872 \u00d7 10 = 8,720, 872 \u00d7 1 = 872. Then 87,200 + 8,720 + 872 = 87,200 + 8,720 = 95,920 + 872 = 96,792. Wait, that's different. Wait, no, that can't be. Wait, 872 \u00d7 100 is 87,200. 872 \u00d7 10 is 8,720. 872 \u00d7 1 is 872. So 87,200 + 8,720 + 872. Let's compute 87,200 + 8,720 first. 87,200 + 8,000 = 95,200. Then +720 = 95,920. Then +872: 95,920 + 800 = 96,720. Then +72 = 96,792. Wait, that's conflicting with the previous result. Wait, but 114 is 100 + 14, not 1 + 10. Wait, no, 114 is 100 + 14, so 100\u00d7872 + 14\u00d7872. So 100\u00d7872 is 87,200, 14\u00d7872 is 12,208. Then 87,200 + 12,208 = 99,408. So why did I get 96,792 before? Because I miscalculated the decomposition. Wait, 14\u00d7872: 10\u00d7872=8,720 and 4\u00d7872=3,488. Then 8,720 + 3,488 = 12,208. Then 87,200 + 12,208 = 99,408. So that's correct. The mistake earlier was when I thought 114 is 1 + 10, which is not. 114 is 100 + 14. So that was my error. So that method gives the same answer. So that's good.\n\nTherefore, all methods lead to 99,408. So I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 114 and 872 is \\boxed{99408}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 114 and 872, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. **Break down 872 into 800, 70, and 2:**\n   \\[\n   114 \\times 872 = 114 \\times (800 + 70 + 2)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 114 by each component:**\n   - \\(114 \\times 800 = 91,200\\)\n   - \\(114 \\times 70 = 7,980\\)\n   - \\(114 \\times 2 = 228\\)\n\n3. **Add the results together:**\n   \\[\n   91,200 + 7,980 + 228\n   \\]\n\n4. **Perform the addition step-by-step:**\n   - First, add \\(91,200\\) and \\(7,980\\):\n     \\[\n     91,200 + 7,980 = 99,180\n     \\]\n   - Then, add \\(228\\) to the result:\n     \\[\n     99,180 + 22