      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 718 and 898. Hmm, let's see. Multiplying two three-digit numbers. Alright, maybe I can do this step by step. Let me recall how to multiply larger numbers. \n\nFirst, I can break down 718 into 700 + 18, and 898 into 800 + 98. Then, maybe use the distributive property. So, (700 + 18) multiplied by (800 + 98). Let me write that out:\n\n718 \u00d7 898 = (700 + 18)(800 + 98)\n\nUsing the distributive property, that would be:\n\n700\u00d7800 + 700\u00d798 + 18\u00d7800 + 18\u00d798\n\nLet me compute each part separately.\n\nFirst, 700\u00d7800. That's straightforward. 700 times 800. Well, 7 times 8 is 56, and then there are four zeros (from 700 and 800), so 560,000.\n\nNext, 700\u00d798. Hmm, 700 times 98. Maybe think of 98 as 100 - 2. So, 700\u00d7100 is 70,000, minus 700\u00d72 which is 1,400. So, 70,000 - 1,400 = 68,600.\n\nThen, 18\u00d7800. Let's see, 10\u00d7800 is 8,000, and 8\u00d7800 is 6,400. So, 8,000 + 6,400 = 14,400.\n\nLastly, 18\u00d798. Again, maybe break this down. 18\u00d7100 is 1,800, minus 18\u00d72 which is 36. So, 1,800 - 36 = 1,764.\n\nNow, add all those four results together:\n\n560,000 (from 700\u00d7800) + 68,600 (from 700\u00d798) + 14,400 (from 18\u00d7800) + 1,764 (from 18\u00d798)\n\nLet me add them step by step.\n\nFirst, 560,000 + 68,600. Let's see, 560,000 + 60,000 is 620,000, then add 8,600 more: 628,600.\n\nNext, add 14,400 to that. 628,600 + 14,400. 628,600 + 10,000 is 638,600, then add 4,400: 638,600 + 4,400 = 643,000.\n\nThen, add 1,764. 643,000 + 1,764. Let's break that down: 643,000 + 1,000 = 644,000, then add 764: 644,000 + 700 = 644,700, then +64 = 644,764.\n\nSo, according to that calculation, the product is 644,764. Let me check if that makes sense. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nMultiply 718 by 898. Let's do it step by step.\n\nFirst, multiply 718 by 8 (the units digit of 898). 718 \u0