cond method. Let me re-examine.\n\nOriginal problem: 578 x 622.\n\nBreaking down 622 into 600 + 20 + 2.\n\nSo:\n\n578 x 600 = 578 x 6 x 100 = 3,468 x 100 = 346,800.\n\n578 x 20 = 11,560.\n\n578 x 2 = 1,156.\n\nAdding them together: 346,800 + 11,560 = 358,360. Then 358,360 + 1,156 = 359,516.\n\nBut when I did the standard multiplication earlier, I had:\n\nFirst partial product: 1,156.\n\nSecond partial product: 115,600.\n\nThird partial product: 34,680.\n\nAdding them: 1,156 + 115,600 = 116,756. Then 116,756 + 34,680 = 151,436. Wait, that's even different. Wait, no, that can't be. Wait, maybe I messed up the alignment in the standard multiplication.\n\nWait, let's do the standard multiplication step by step.\n\nMultiply 578 by 622:\n\n       578\n     x 622\n     -------\n       1156   (578 x 2)\n      3570     (578 x 20, shifted one position left)\n     33450      (578 x 600, shifted two positions left)\n     -------\n     359,516   (Total)\n\nWait, so the third partial product is 33,450, not 34,680. Wait, 578 x 600 is 578 x 6 x 100. 578 x 6 is 3,468. Then 3,468 x 100 is 346,800. So that's 346,800. But in the standard multiplication, when we multiply by the hundreds digit (6), we need to shift the previous partial products. Wait, maybe I confused the digits.\n\nWait, let's do the standard multiplication properly.\n\nStarting with the rightmost digit of the multiplier (622), which is 2:\n\nMultiply 578 by 2: 1,156. Write that down.\n\nNext digit is 2 (tens place). Multiply 578 by 2, get 1,156. Since it's the tens place, shift one position to the left: 11,560.\n\nNext digit is 6 (hundreds place). Multiply 578 by 6: 3,468. Since it's the hundreds place, shift two positions to the left: 346,800.\n\nNow add them all together:\n\n1,156\n\n+11,560\n\n+346,800\n\nLet's add 1,156 + 11,560 first. 1,156 + 11,560 = 12,716.\n\nThen add 12,716 + 346,800. Let's compute that:\n\n12,716\n\n+346,800\n\n= 359,516.\n\nSo the total is 359,516. But earlier, when I broke down 622 into 600 + 20 + 2, I got 359,516. So that's consistent. But when I first did the standard multiplication, I thought the third partial product was 34,680, which was wrong. Probably a miscalculation. So the correct total is 359,516.\n\nBut wait, when I did the first calculation, I thought the answer was 47,396, which is way too low. So that was a mistake. Then when I broke it down, I got 359,516, which seems correct. Let me verify with another method.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the calculator method. Let me compute 578 x 622.\n\nFirst, 578 x 600 = 346,800.\n\n578 x 20 = 11,560.\n\n578 x 2 = 1,156.\n\nAdding them: 346,800 + 11,560 = 358,360. Then 358,360 + 1,156 = 359,516. So that's the same result. So that must be the correct answer.\n\nBut let me check with another approach. Let's use the formula (a + b)(c + d) = ac + ad + bc + bd, but maybe that's complicating. Alternatively, maybe use the difference of squares or something, but 578 and 622 don't seem to form a nice pair. Alternatively, approximate.\n\nWait, 578 is approximately 600 - 22, and 622 is 600 + 22. So (600 - 22)(600 + 22) = 600\u00b2 - (22)\u00b2 = 360,000 - 484 = 359,516. Oh! That's clever. So that's another way to compute it. So that confirms the answer is 359,516.\n\nTherefore, the product of 578 and 622 is 359,516.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 578 and 622 is \\boxed{359516}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 578 and 622, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n578 \\times 622 = 578 \\times (600 + 20 + 2)\n\\]\n\nBreaking this down into smaller parts:\n\n1. Calculate \\(578 \\times 600\\):\n   \\[\n   578 \\times 600 = 578 \\times 6 \\times 100 = 3468 \\times 100 = 346800\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(578 \\times 20\\):\n   \\[\n   578 \\times 20 = 578 \\times 2 \\times 10 = 1156 \\times 10 = 11560\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(578 \\times 2\\):\n   \\[\n   578 \\times 2 = 1156\n   \\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n346800 + 11560 + 1156\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(346800\\) and \\(11560\\):\n\\[\n346800 + 11560 = 358360\n\\]\n\nThen, add the result to \\(1156\\):\n\\[\n358360 + 1156 = 359516\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 578 and 622 is \\(\\boxed{359516}\\).",
