      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 191 and 871. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two-digit numbers can sometimes be done by breaking them down into smaller parts, but I'm not sure if that's the best approach here. Maybe I should just do the standard multiplication method step by step. Let me try that.\n\nFirst, let me write down the numbers:\n\n      191\n    x 871\n    -------\n\nAlright, starting with the multiplication of 191 by 1, which is the ones place. That should be straightforward. 191 x 1 = 191. But wait, in multiplication, each digit is multiplied by the corresponding digit in the other number, starting from the right, and then we shift them. So actually, the first step is to multiply 191 by 1 (the ones place of 871), which is 191. Then we write that down, but shifted one position to the left because it's the ones place. So now we have:\n\n      191\n    x 871\n    -------\n\n      191\n    0 0 0 (but these zeros are just placeholders)\n    0 0 0\n    -------\n\nWait, maybe I should structure it properly. Let me recall the standard multiplication algorithm. When multiplying by a three-digit number, you first multiply by the ones digit, then the tens digit, then the hundreds digit, each time shifting the result one position to the left. Then you add all the partial products together.\n\nSo, breaking down 871 into its digits: 8 (hundreds place), 7 (tens place), 1 (ones place). Therefore, we'll have three partial products:\n\n1. 191 x 1 = 191 (shifted one position left)\n2. 191 x 7 = 1337 (shifted two positions left)\n3. 191 x 8 = 1528 (shifted three positions left)\n\nThen we add them all up. Let me verify each step.\n\nFirst, multiplying 191 by 1 is indeed 191. Then, multiplying 191 by 7. Let's compute that:\n\n191 x 7. Let's do 200 x 7 = 1400, then subtract 9 x 7 = 63. So 1400 - 63 = 1337. That's correct. Then 191 x 8. Let's compute that:\n\n191 x 8. 200 x 8 = 1600, subtract 9 x 8 = 72. So 1600 - 72 = 1528. Correct.\n\nNow, writing these partial products with appropriate shifts:\n\n      191\n    x 871\n    -------\n\n      191  (shifted one left)\n     1337   (shifted two left)\n    1528    (shifted three left)\n    ---------\n\nBut when written out, the shifted numbers would be:\n\n191\n1337\n1528\n------\n?\n\nSo adding them up. Let's align them properly:\n\n      191\n    1337\n   1528\n    -------\n\nWait, actually, when shifted left by one, two, three positions, the alignment would be:\n\nThe first partial product (191 x 1) is 191, which is in the ones place.\n\nThe second partial product (191 x 7) is 1337, shifted two places left, so it's in the tens and hundreds place.\n\nThe third partial product (191 x 8) is 1528, shifted three places left, so it's in the thousands and ten thousands place.\n\nSo when written out:\n\n      191\n    x 871\n    -------\n\n      191\n     000\n    000\n    -------\n\nWait, maybe I need to write each partial product with leading zeros to account for the shifts. Let's see:\n\nFirst partial product: 191 (no shift, since it's the ones place)\nSecond partial product: 1337 shifted two places left, so it's 133700 (but wait, 1337 shifted two places left would be 133700? Wait, no. Wait, shifting left by two places would mean multiplying by 100. So 1337 x 100 = 133700. Similarly, shifting left by three places would be 1528 x 1000 = 1,528,000.\n\nBut when adding them together, we need to align the digits properly. Let me try writing them out:\n\nFirst, write down the numbers as they are:\n\n      191\n    x 871\n    -------\n\n      191  (ones place)\n     1337   (tens and hundreds place)\n    1528    (thousands and ten thousands place)\n\nBut to add them, we need to line up the digits by their place values. So:\n\n      191\n    1337\n   1528\n    -------\n\nBut in terms of place value:\n\n- The first number (191) is in the ones place.\n- The second number (1337) is in the hundreds and tens place (since it's shifted two places left).\n- The third number (1528) is in the thousands and ten thousands place (shifted three places left).\n\nSo when written out:\n\n      191\n    1337\n   1528\n    -------\n\nBut to add them, we need to consider each digit's place:\n\nLet me write each partial product with their respective shifts:\n\n1. 191 (1's place)\n2. 1337 shifted two places left: so it's 133700? Wait, no. Wait, shifting left by two places is equivalent to multiplying by 100. So 191 x 7 = 1337, then shifted two places left becomes 1337 x 100 = 133700. Similarly, 191 x 8 = 1528, shifted three places left is 1528 x 1000 = 1,528,000.\n\nBut when adding these three numbers: 191, 133700, and 1,528,000.\n\nBut adding them together:\n\nFirst, 133700 + 1,528,000 = 1,661,700. Then add 191: 1,661,700 + 191 = 1,661,891.\n\nWait, is that correct? Let me check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the shifting. Let me verify each step again.\n\nOriginal multiplication:\n\n      191\n    x 871\n    -------\n\nMultiplying 191 by 1 (units place of 871):\n\n191 x 1 = 191. This is the units digit of the product, so we write 191.\n\nMultiplying 191 by 7 (tens place of 871):\n\n191 x 7 = 1337. This is the tens and hundreds digit, so we need to shift two places to the left. So 1337 becomes 133700 when shifted two places left.\n\nMultiplying 191 by 8 (hundreds place of 871):\n\n191 x 8 = 1528. This is the thousands and ten thousands digit, so we shift three places left. 1528 becomes 1,528,000 when shifted three places left.\n\nNow, adding these three numbers:\n\n191 (which is 0191 if we consider it as a three-digit number)\n133700 (which is 1 337 00)\n1,528,000 (which is 1,528,000)\n\nBut adding them:\n\nFirst, 133700 + 1,528,000 = 1,661,700. Then adding 191 gives 1,661,700 + 191 = 1,661,891.\n\nWait, but let me check this addition step by step.\n\n1,661,700 + 191:\n\n1,661,700 + 100 = 1,661,800\n\n1,661,800 + 90 = 1,661,890\n\n1,661,890 + 1 = 1,661,891. Yes, that's correct.\n\nBut let me verify using another method to be sure. Maybe the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the distributive property:\n\n191 x 871 = (200 - 9) x 871 = 200x871 - 9x871.\n\nCompute 200x871 = 174,200.\n\nCompute 9x871: 9x800=7,200; 9x70=630; 9x1=9. So total 7,200 + 630 + 9 = 7,839.\n\nTherefore, 174,200 - 7,839 = ?\n\n174,200 - 7,000 = 167,200\n\n167,200 - 839 = 166,361.\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result. Hmm, so now I have two different answers. That's concerning. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let's check the second method again.\n\nFirst, 191 is 200 - 9. So 200*871 = 174,200. Then 9*871: 800*9=7,200; 70*9=630; 1*9=9. So 7,200 + 630 + 9 = 7,839. Then 174,200 - 7,839. Let's compute that.\n\n174,200 minus 7,000 is 167,200. Then subtract 839 more: 167,200 - 800 = 166,400; 166,400 - 39 = 166,361. So according to this method, the answer is 166,361.\n\nBut according to the first method, adding the partial products, I got 1,661,891. These are vastly different. There must be a mistake here.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the shifting in the first method. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal problem: 191 x 871.\n\nBreaking down 871 into 800 + 70 + 1.\n\nSo:\n\n191 x 800 = ?\n\n191 x 8 = 1,528. Then 1,528 x 100 = 152,800.\n\n191 x 70 = 191 x 7 x 10 = 1,337 x 10 = 13,370.\n\n191 x 1 = 191.\n\nSo adding them up: 152,800 + 13,370 + 191.\n\n152,800 + 13,370 = 166,170. Then 166,170 + 191 = 166,361. Ah! There we go. So that's the same result as the second method. So where did I go wrong in the first method?\n\nWait, in the first method, I considered the partial products shifted left by 1, 2, 3 positions. But perhaps I misaligned them when adding. Let me see.\n\nFirst partial product: 191 x 1 = 191. This is the units digit, so it's 191.\n\nSecond partial product: 191 x 7 = 1,337. This is the tens and hundreds digit, so shifted two places left, which would be 13,370. Wait, but 1,337 shifted two places left is 13,370. Then third partial product: 191 x 8 = 1,528. Shifted three places left is 152,800.\n\nSo the three partial products are:\n\n191, 13,370, 152,800.\n\nAdding them together:\n\n191 + 13,370 = 13,561\n\n13,561 + 152,800 = 166,361. Ah! There we go. So I must have made a mistake in the alignment earlier. When I first tried adding them, I thought the partial products were 191, 1337, 1528, but shifted incorrectly. But actually, when shifted left by 1, 2, 3 positions, the numbers should be 191, 13,370, 152,800. Then adding those gives 166,361. So that's correct.\n\nTherefore, both methods give the same result: 166,361. So the correct answer is 166,361.\n\nBut let me confirm once more with another method. Let's do the standard multiplication step by step.\n\nMultiplying 191 by 871:\n\nFirst, multiply 191 by 1: 191.\n\nThen multiply 191 by 70: 191 x 7 x 10 = 1,337 x 10 = 13,370.\n\nThen multiply 191 by 800: 191 x 8 x 100 = 1,528 x 100 = 152,800.\n\nNow add them up:\n\n191 + 13,370 = 13,561\n\n13,561 + 152,800 = 166,361.\n\nYes, that's consistent. So the product is 166,361.\n\nAlternatively, using a calculator to verify:\n\n191 x 871. Let's compute 191 x 800 = 152,800; 191 x 70 = 13,370; 191 x 1 = 191. Adding those: 152,800 + 13,370 = 166,170; 166,170 + 191 = 166,361. Correct.\n\nTherefore, the product of 191 and 871 is 166,361.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 191 and 871 is \\boxed{166361}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 191 and 871, we can break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\n1. Calculate \\( 191 \\times 800 \\):\n   \\[\n   191 \\times 800 = 191 \\times 8 \\times 100 = 1528 \\times 100 = 152800\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\( 191 \\times 70 \\):\n   \\[\n   191 \\times 70 = 191 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 1337 \\times 10 = 13370\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\( 191 \\times 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   191 \\times 1 = 191\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the results of the three calculations together:\n   \\[\n   152800 + 13370 + 191\n   \\]\n\n5. Perform the addition step-by-step:\n   \\[\n   152800 + 13370 = 166170\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   166170 + 191 = 166361\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 191 and 871 is \\(\\boxed{166361}\\).",
