7 + 2 (carry) = 11. Write down 11.\n\nSo the result is 94,943. Oh! Wait, I must have misaligned the digits when adding earlier. So in the standard multiplication, adding 263, 1,578, and 7,890 gives 94,943. So that's correct. My mistake earlier was in the initial addition steps where I thought the sum was 9,731, but that was because I didn't properly line up the digits. So the correct total is 94,943.\n\nTherefore, all methods lead to the same result. So I can be confident that the product of 263 and 361 is 94,943.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 263 and 361 is \\boxed{94943}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 263 and 361, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n263 \\times 361 = (260 + 3)(360 + 1)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(260 + 3)(360 + 1) = 260 \\times 360 + 260 \\times 1 + 3 \\times 360 + 3 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n1. \\(260 \\times 360 = 93,600\\)\n2. \\(260 \\times 1 = 260\\)\n3. \\(3 \\times 360 = 1,080\\)\n4. \\(3 \\times 1 = 3\\)\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n93,600 + 260 + 1,080 + 3\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(93,600\\) and \\(260\\):\n\n\\[\n93,600 + 260 = 93,860\n\\]\n\nNext, add \\(1,080\\):\n\n\\[\n93,860 + 1,080 = 94,940\n\\]\n\nFinally, add \\(3\\):\n\n\\[\n94,940 + 3 = 94,943\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 263 and 361 is \\(\\boxed{94943}\\).",
