rrect?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the breakdown method. Let me check again.\n\nWait, 200*800 = 160,000\n\n200*20 = 4,000\n\n200*6 = 1,200\n\n9*800 = 7,200\n\n9*20 = 180\n\n9*6 = 54\n\nAdding them:\n\n160,000 + 4,000 = 164,000\n\n164,000 + 1,200 = 165,200\n\n165,200 + 7,200 = 172,400\n\n172,400 + 180 = 172,580\n\n172,580 + 54 = 172,634. Correct.\n\nBut according to standard multiplication, it's 22,154. So, which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the standard multiplication. Let me compute 209 x 826 using another method.\n\nLet me compute 209 x 826:\n\nFirst, compute 200 x 826 = 165,200\n\nThen, compute 9 x 826 = 7,434\n\nAdd them together: 165,200 + 7,434 = 172,634. Correct.\n\nTherefore, the breakdown method is correct, and the standard multiplication must have an error. So, where is the mistake in the standard multiplication?\n\nWait, in the standard multiplication, when I wrote down the partial products, I think I added them incorrectly. Let me check again.\n\nFirst partial product: 1,254 (units)\n\nSecond partial product: 4,180 (tens)\n\nThird partial product: 16,720 (hundreds)\n\nBut when adding, the second partial product (4,180) is actually 4,180, which is 4,180. The third partial product (16,720) is 16,720. The first is 1,254. So, adding them:\n\n1,254 + 4,180 = 5,434\n\n5,434 + 16,720 = 22,154. But according to the breakdown method, it's 172,634. So, there's a problem here.\n\nWait, unless I made a mistake in the standard multiplication. Let me check the multiplication by the hundreds digit again.\n\n209 x 800 = 167,200. Correct.\n\n209 x 20 = 4,180. Correct.\n\n209 x 6 = 1,254. Correct.\n\nAdding them: 167,200 + 4,180 = 171,380; 171,380 + 1,254 = 172,634. Correct.\n\nTherefore, the standard multiplication must have an error in the way the partial products are added. Let me check the alignment again.\n\nWait, when multiplying by the hundreds digit (8), the partial product should be 1,672, but shifted two places to the left, making it 16,