      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 640 and 272. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can sometimes be broken down into smaller parts to make it easier. Maybe I can use some kind of decomposition here. Let me think.\n\nFirst, let me write down the numbers: 640 and 272. If I multiply them directly, I might get a big number, but maybe there's a smarter way. Wait, 640 is 64 times 10, right? And 272 is 27 times 10 plus 2. Maybe I can use distributive property here. Let me try that.\n\nSo, 640 times 272 can be written as (64 * 10) * (27 * 10 + 2). Let me expand that. First, multiply 64 by 27, then 64 by 10, and then 64 by 2. Then add all those together. Wait, no, actually, since it's (64*10)*(27*10 + 2), that would be 64*10*27*10 + 64*10*2. Which simplifies to 64*27*100 + 64*20. Hmm, that might work.\n\nLet me compute 64*27 first. 64 times 20 is 1280, and 64 times 7 is 448. So adding those together, 1280 + 448 = 1728. Then multiply by 100, so 1728*100 = 172,800. Then 64*20 is 1,280. So adding 172,800 + 1,280 = 174,080. Wait, is that right? Let me check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can break down 272 into 200 + 70 + 2. Then multiply each part by 640. Let's try that. 640*200 = 128,000. 640*70 = 44,800. 640*2 = 1,280. Then add them up: 128,000 + 44,800 = 172,800; 172,800 + 1,280 = 174,080. Okay, same result. So that seems consistent.\n\nBut wait, maybe there's another way. Let me see. 640 is 6.4 thousand, and 272 is 2.72 thousand. Multiplying those gives 6.4 * 2.72 = 17.408 thousand, which is 174,080. Oh, that's the same answer. So that works too. So regardless of the method, I get 174,080. Let me verify with a calculator to be sure.\n\nBut since I don't have a calculator, maybe I can check by adding 640 and 272 first. Wait, no, addition isn't helpful here. Alternatively, maybe use another multiplication technique. Let's do the standard multiplication method.\n\nMultiply 640 by 272:\n\nFirst, write 640\ntimes 272.\n\nStart with the units digit: 2. Multiply 640 by 2: 1280. Write down 2 (since 1280 has three digits, but we take the last digit for the units place). Wait, no, actually, in standard multiplication, you write the partial products and add them up.\n\nLet me set it up:\n\n      640\n    x 272\n    ------\n      1280  (640 x 2)\n    4480    (640 x 70, shifted one position to the left)\n  172800   (640 x 200, shifted two positions to the left)\n    ------\n  174080   (sum all partial products)\n\nWait, let me check that again. When multiplying by the tens digit (70), you have to shift the partial product one place to the left. So 640 x 70 is 44,800. Then, writing that with one space in front: 44800. Then 640 x 200 is 128,000, which is written as 128000. Then adding them up:\n\nFirst, 128,000\n+ 44,800\n= 172,800\nThen add 1,280 (from 640 x 2)\n172,800 + 1,280 = 174,080. Yep, same result.\n\nSo all methods lead to 174,080. Therefore, the product of 640 and 272 is 174,080. I think that's correct. Let me just do a quick check with another method. For example, using the fact that 640 x 272 = (600 + 40) x (200 + 70 + 2). Let's expand that:\n\n600*200 = 120,000\n\n600*70 = 42,000\n\n600*2 = 1,200\n\n40*200 = 8,000\n\n40*70 = 2,800\n\n40*2 = 80\n\nNow add all those together:\n\n120,000 + 42,000 = 162,000\n\n162,000 + 1,200 = 163,200\n\n163,200 + 8,000 = 171,200\n\n171,200 + 2,800 = 174,000\n\n174,000 + 80 = 174,080. Same answer again. Okay, so definitely 174,080. I think that's solid.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 640 and 272 is \\boxed{174080}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 640 and 272, we can use the distributive property and break down the multiplication into smaller parts.\n\nFirst, we can decompose 272 into 200 + 70 + 2. Then, multiply each part by 640 and sum the results:\n\n1. Calculate \\( 640 \\times 200 \\):\n   \\[\n   640 \\times 200 = 128000\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\( 640 \\times 70 \\):\n   \\[\n   640 \\times 70 = 44800\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\( 640 \\times 2 \\):\n   \\[\n   640 \\times 2 = 1280\n   \\]\n\n4. Sum the results:\n   \\[\n   128000 + 44800 + 1280 = 174080\n   \\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. Multiply 640 by 2:\n   \\[\n   640 \\times 2 = 1280\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 640 by 70 (shifted one position to the left):\n   \\[\n   640 \\times 70 = 44800\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 640 by 200 (shifted two positions to the left):\n   \\[\n   640 \\times 200 = 128000\n   \\]\n\n4. Sum the partial products:\n   \\[\n   128000 + 44800 + 1280 = 174080\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 640 and 272 is \\(\\boxed{174080}\\).",
