he original problem is 667 \u00d7 304. So, that approach is not applicable here. Therefore, my initial thought to use that formula was incorrect because 304 is not 300 - 4. Wait, 304 is 300 + 4, so the formula approach was trying to represent 304 as 300 - 4, which is not correct. Therefore, that method is invalid here. So, that was a wrong approach. Therefore, the correct answer is 202,768.\n\nSo, the error came from trying to apply a formula that didn't align with the original numbers. Therefore, better to stick with the direct multiplication or breaking down into smaller parts.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another method. Let's try adding 667 and 304 first, but that might not help. Alternatively, use the fact that 667 \u00d7 304 = 667 \u00d7 (300 + 4) which we already did. So, 200,100 + 2,668 = 202,768. So, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, use the standard multiplication algorithm again. Let me write it out:\n\n        667\n      \u00d7 304\n      --------\n\nFirst, multiply 667 by 4:\n\n667\n\u00d7  4\n-----\n2668\n\nThen multiply 667 by 0 (tens place):\n\n   0\n-----\n   0\n\nThen multiply 667 by 3 (hundreds place):\n\n667\n\u00d7  3\n-----\n2001\n\nNow, add them up:\n\n  2668\n+  0\n+2001\n-------\n202,768\n\nYes, same result. So, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator to verify. But since I don't have a calculator here, but I can check with another multiplication method. Let's try multiplying 667 \u00d7 304 using the standard algorithm step by step.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 \u00d7 667 = 2,668. Write down 2,668.\n\n0 \u00d7 667 = 0. Write down 0.\n\n3 \u00d7 667 = 2,001. Write down 2,001.\n\nAdding them up gives 202,768. Yep, same as before.\n\nTherefore, I can be confident that the product of 667 and 304 is 202,768.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 667 and 304 is \\boxed{202768}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 667 and 304, we can break down 304 into 300 and 4, then multiply each part separately and add the results.\n\nFirst, calculate \\(667 \\times 300\\):\n\\[\n667 \\times 300 = 667 \\times 3 \\times 100 = 2001 \\times 100 = 200100\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(667 \\times 4\\):\n\\[\n667 \\times 4 = 2668\n\\]\n\nAdd the two results together:\n\\[\n200100 + 2668 = 202768\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  667 \\\\\n\\times 304 \\\\\n\\hline\n  2668 \\quad \\text{(667 \\times 4)} \\\\\n   0 \\quad \\text{(667 \\times 0)} \\\\\n2001 \\quad \\text{(667 \\times 3)} \\\\\n\\hline\n202768 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 667 and 304 is \\(\\boxed{202768}\\).",
