according to the standard multiplication, it's 38,739. That's a problem. There must be a mistake in the standard multiplication.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the alignment. Let me try again.\n\nMultiplying 349 by 511:\n\nFirst, write 349 \u00d7 1 (units digit):\n\n   349\n\nThen, multiply 349 by 1 (tens digit), which is 3490, shifted one position to the left:\n\n  3490\n\nThen, multiply 349 by 5 (hundreds digit), which is 34900, shifted two positions to the left:\n\n 34900\n\nNow, adding these three numbers:\n\n   349\n\n  3490\n\n 34900\n\n------------\n\nLet me add them step by step.\n\nFirst, add 349 and 3490:\n\n349 + 3490 = 3,839.\n\nThen, add 34,900 to 3,839:\n\n3,839 + 34,900. Let's compute 3,839 + 34,900:\n\n3,839 + 34,900. Let's break it down:\n\n3,839 + 30,000 = 33,839\n\n33,839 + 4,900 = 38,739.\n\nWait, so according to this, the total is 38,739. But according to the other method, it's 178,339. There's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first place. Let me check with a calculator. Let me compute 349 \u00d7 511.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute 349 \u00d7 511 using another approach.\n\nAlternatively, note that 511 is 500 + 11. So 349 \u00d7 511 = 349 \u00d7 500 + 349 \u00d7 11.\n\nCompute 349 \u00d7 500 = 174,500.\n\nCompute 349 \u00d7 11. 349 \u00d7 10 = 3,490. 349 \u00d7 1 = 349. So 3,490 + 349 = 3,839.\n\nThen, 174,500 + 3,839 = 178,339. So that's 178,339. So that's the correct answer.\n\nTherefore, my initial calculation using the standard multiplication was wrong. So where did I go wrong?\n\nAh, I see. When I did the standard multiplication, I added 349 + 3490 + 34900, but that's incorrect. Because in standard multiplication, when you multiply by the hundreds digit, you shift the partial product two places to the left, but in reality, the digits are 34900, which is 34,900. But when adding, we need to align the digits properly.\n\nWait, let's write all three partial products with proper alignment:\n\n 