te the numbers vertically:\n\n      349\n\n     3490\n\n    34900\n\n------------\n\nNow, adding them:\n\nStart from the rightmost digit:\n\nUnits place: 9 (from 349) + 0 (from 3490) + 0 (from 34900) = 9\n\nTens place: 4 (from 349) + 9 (from 3490) + 0 (from 34900) = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place: 3 (from 349) + 4 (from 3490) + 0 (from 34900) = 7, plus the carried 1 = 8.\n\nThousands place: 4 (from 349) + 9 (from 3490) = 13. Write down 3, carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 (from 349) + 3 (from 3490) = 6, plus the carried 1 = 7.\n\nSo the total is 78,339. Wait, 78,339. But according to the other method, it's 178,339. So this is conflicting again.\n\nWait, this is getting me really confused. Let me check with a calculator. Let me compute 349 \u00d7 511.\n\n349 \u00d7 500 = 174,500\n\n349 \u00d7 10 = 3,490\n\n349 \u00d7 1 = 349\n\nAdding them up: 174,500 + 3,490 = 178, 990? Wait, 174,500 + 3,490. 174,500 + 3,000 = 177,500. 177,500 + 490 = 177,990. Then 177,990 + 349 = 178,339. So that's 178,339.\n\nBut when I added the partial products using the standard multiplication method, I got 78,339. Which is way off. So there's a mistake here. What's going on?\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the alignment of the partial products. Let me try again.\n\nWhen multiplying 349 by 511, the partial products should be:\n\n1. 349 \u00d7 1 = 349 (units place)\n\n2. 349 \u00d7 1 = 349 shifted one position to the left (tens place): 3490\n\n3. 349 \u00d7 5 = 1,745 shifted two positions to the left (hundreds place): 34,900\n\nNow, adding these three numbers:\n\n349\n\n3490\n\n34900\n\nBut when adding, we need to make sure that each subsequent number is shifted appropriately. Wait, but in reality, when you do multiplication, each partial product is shifted left by the number of digits in the multiplier. So for 511, which is three digits, each partial product is shifted left by 2, 1, and 0 places? Wait, no. Wait, the first partial product is for the units digit (1's