nds, hundreds, tens, units)\n\nWait, no. Wait, 3490 is 3 thousands, 4 hundreds, 9 tens, 0 units.\n\n34900 is 3 ten-thousands, 4 thousands, 9 hundreds, 0 tens, 0 units.\n\nSo when adding:\n\n349 (hundreds, tens, units)\n\n3490 (thousands, hundreds, tens, units)\n\n34900 (ten-thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, units)\n\nSo when written vertically:\n\n      349\n\n     3490\n\n    34900\n\nBut when adding, the digits are:\n\nUnits: 9 + 0 + 0 = 9\n\nTens: 4 + 9 + 0 = 13 (write 3, carry 1)\n\nHundreds: 3 + 4 + 0 = 7 + 1 = 8\n\nThousands: 4 + 9 = 13 (write 3, carry 1)\n\nTen-thousands: 3 + 3 = 6 + 1 = 7\n\nSo total is 78,339. But according to the other method, it's 178,339. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first place. Let me check with another approach. Let's compute 349 \u00d7 511.\n\nAlternatively, note that 511 is 500 + 10 + 1. So:\n\n349 \u00d7 511 = 349 \u00d7 (500 + 10 + 1) = 349\u00d7500 + 349\u00d710 + 349\u00d71.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n349 \u00d7 500: 349 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 100 = 1,745 \u00d7 100 = 174,500.\n\n349 \u00d7 10: 3,490.\n\n349 \u00d7 1: 349.\n\nAdding them up: 174,500 + 3,490 = 178, 990? Wait, 174,500 + 3,490. Let's compute 174,500 + 3,000 = 177,500. Then 177,500 + 490 = 177,990. Then 177,990 + 349 = 178,339. So that's 178,339.\n\nBut according to the standard multiplication method, adding the partial products gives 78,339. So there's a conflict here. Therefore, one of the methods must be wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps the standard multiplication method is not being applied correctly. Let me check the alignment again.\n\nStandard multiplication steps:\n\n1. Multiply the multiplicand (349) by each digit of the multiplier (511) starting from the right.\n\n2. The first digit (1) is multiplied: 349 \u00d7 1 = 349. This is the units digit.\n\n3. The second digit (1) is multiplied and shifted one position to the left: 349 \u00d7 1 = 3490. This is the tens digit.\n\n4. The third digit (5) is multiplied and shifted two positions to the left: 349 \u00d7 5 = 1,745. This is the hundreds digit.\n\nNow, adding these three numbers:\n\n349\n\n3490\n\n17,450 (Wait, 34900)\n\nWait, 34900 is 34,900. So 349 + 3490 + 34,900.\n\nBut when adding:\n\n349 + 3490 = 3,839\n\n3,839 + 34,900 = 38,739.\n\nBut according to the other method, it's 178,339. So which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe the problem is that in the standard multiplication, the partial products are added as they are, but in reality, when you do multiplication step by step, each partial product corresponds to a different place value. Let me check with the actual multiplication.\n\nLet me compute 349 \u00d7 511 manually.\n\nFirst, multiply 349 by 1: 349.\n\nThen, multiply 349 by 10: 3,490.\n\nThen, multiply 349 by 100: 34,900.\n\nNow, adding these three numbers:\n\n349 + 3,490 + 34,900.\n\nBut 349 + 3,490 = 3,839.\n\n3,839 + 34,900 = 38,739.\n\nBut according to the other method, it's 178,339. So which is correct?\n\nWait, this is impossible. There must be a mistake here. Let me check with a calculator.\n\nLet me compute 349 \u00d7 511.\n\n349 \u00d7 500 = 174,500\n\n349 \u00d7 10 = 3,490\n\n349 \u00d7 1 = 349\n\nTotal: 174,500 + 3,490 = 178, 990? Wait, 174,500 + 3,490. 174,500 + 3,000 = 177,500. 177,500 + 490 = 177,990. Then 177,990 + 349 = 178,339.\n\nYes, so 178,339 is correct. Therefore, the standard multiplication method must have been applied incorrectly. So where is the mistake?\n\nAh! I think I see the problem. When I did the standard multiplication, I wrote the partial products as:\n\n349\n\n3490\n\n34900\n\nBut in reality, when you multiply by the hundreds place (5), you need to shift the partial product two places to the left, which would be 34900. However, when adding, the alignment is different. Wait, but when you do the multiplication step by step, each partial product is for a different place value. Let me think.\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion arises because when you do the multiplication step by step, each partial product is for a differen