      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 110 and 905. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done step by step, but maybe there's a quicker way to do it mentally. Let me think. \n\nFirst, maybe I can break down 110 into 100 and 10. So, 110 is 100 + 10. Then, if I multiply 905 by 100, that's straightforward. 905 times 100 is 90,500. Then, 905 times 10 is 9,050. So, if I add those two results together, 90,500 + 9,050. Let me check that. 90,500 plus 9,000 would be 99,500, and then plus 50 more is 99,550. Wait, is that right? Let me verify.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard multiplication method. Let's try that. Multiplying 110 by 905. Let's ignore the 110 for a moment and first multiply 100 by 905. That's 90,500. Then, multiply 10 by 905, which is 9,050. Adding those together gives 90,500 + 9,050. Let me add them step by step. 90,500 + 9,000 is 99,500, and then adding the remaining 50 gives 99,550. So that's the same result as before. \n\nWait, but maybe there's another way to check. Let me use the distributive property again. 110 times 905 can be written as 100 times 905 plus 10 times 905. Which is exactly what I did before. So that's 90,500 + 9,050. Hmm, seems consistent. \n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 905 into smaller parts. Let's see, 905 is 900 + 5. So, 110 times 900 is 99,000, and 110 times 5 is 550. Then, adding those together: 99,000 + 550. That's 99,550. Oh, same answer! So that's another way to do it. \n\nWait, so both methods give me 99,550. That seems solid. Let me just confirm with a calculator to be sure. But since I don't have a calculator here, maybe I can check with another multiplication technique. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the fact that 110 is 10 times 11. So, 10 times 11 is 110, so 110 times 905 is 11 times 905 times 10. Which is the same as (11 times 905) times 10. Let's compute 11 times 905 first. 11 times 900 is 9,900, and 11 times 5 is 55. So, 9,900 + 55 is 9,955. Then, multiplying by 10 gives 99,550. Yep, same result again. \n\nSo, all different methods are leading me to 99,550. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct. Let me just recap to make sure I didn't make any arithmetic errors. \n\nFirst method: 110 = 100 + 10. 100*905=90,500; 10*905=9,050. Sum: 90,500 + 9,050 = 99,550. \n\nSecond method: 905 = 900 + 5. 110*900=99,000; 110*5=550. Sum: 99,000 + 550 = 99,550. \n\nThird method: 110 = 10*11. 11*905=9,955; 9,955*10=99,550. \n\nAll three methods give the same result. So, unless I made the same mistake in all three calculations, which is possible but unlikely, the answer should be 99,550. \n\nWait, let me check the second method again. 110 times 900. 110*900. 110*9 is 990, so 110*900 is 990*10=9,900. Correct. Then 110*5. 110*5 is 550. Correct. Then 9,900 + 550. 9,900 + 500 is 10,400, minus 50 is 9,950? Wait, wait, hold on. Wait, 9,900 + 550. Let me do this again. 9,900 + 500 is 10,400. Then, 10,400 + 50 is 10,450. Wait, that's different from before. Wait, no, wait. Wait, 9,900 + 550. Let's break 550 into 500 + 50. So, 9,900 + 500 = 10,400. Then, 10,400 + 50 = 10,450. Wait, but earlier I thought it was 99,550. Wait, no, wait. Wait, 9,900 + 550. 9,900 + 500 is 10,400. Then, 10,400 + 50 is 10,450. Wait, but 10,450 is 10,450. But 9,900 + 550 is 10,450. But 10,450 is 10,450. Wait, but 99,550 is 99,550. Wait, no, wait. Wait, 9,900 + 550. Let me compute 9,900 + 550. 9,900 + 500 is 10,400. Then, 10,400 + 50 is 10,450. Wait, but 10,450 is 10,450. But 9,900 + 550 is 10,450. But 10,450 is 10,450. Wait, but 99,550 is 99,550. Wait, no, 9,900 + 550 is 10,450. Wait, but 10,450 is 10,450. Wait, but 9,900 + 550 is 10,450. Wait, but 10,450 is 10,450. Wait, but 9,900 + 550 is 10,450. Wait, but 10,450 is 10,450. Wait, but 9,900 + 550 is 10,450. Wait, but 10,450 is 10,450. Wait, but 9,900 + 550 is 10,450. Wait, but 10,450 is 10,450. Wait, but 9,900 + 550 is 10,450. Wait, but 10,450 is 10,450. Wait, but 9,900 + 550 is 10,450. Wait, but 10,450 