      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 425 and 131. Hmm, let's see. I remember that multiplying two numbers can be done step by step, but maybe there's a quicker way to do it mentally. Let me think. \n\nFirst, maybe I can break down 131 into smaller parts. I know that 131 is 100 plus 30 plus 1. So, 100 + 30 is 130, plus 1 is 131. So, if I can multiply 425 by 100, 425 by 30, and 425 by 1 separately, then add all those results together, that should give me the total product. That seems manageable.\n\nLet me start with 425 times 100. Well, multiplying by 100 is easy, you just add two zeros. So 425 times 100 is 42,500. Okay, that's straightforward.\n\nNext, 425 times 30. Hmm, 425 times 10 is 4,250. So if I multiply that by 3, it should be 4,250 times 3. Let me calculate that. 4,000 times 3 is 12,000, and 250 times 3 is 750. So adding those together, 12,000 + 750 is 12,750. So 425 times 30 is 12,750. Got that.\n\nNow, 425 times 1. That's simple, just 425. So adding all three parts together: 42,500 (from the 100) + 12,750 (from the 30) + 425 (from the 1). Let me add them step by step.\n\nFirst, 42,500 + 12,750. Let's see, 42,500 + 10,000 is 52,500. Then add 2,750 more. 52,500 + 2,750. Hmm, 52,500 + 2,000 is 54,500. Then add 750: 54,500 + 750 = 55,250. So that's the sum of the three parts. Then add the last 425. So 55,250 + 425. Let's do that. 55,250 + 400 is 55,650. Then add 25 more: 55,650 + 25 = 55,675. So the total product is 55,675. \n\nWait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Sometimes when adding multiple numbers, it's easy to slip up. Let me verify each step.\n\nFirst, 425 x 100 = 42,500. That's correct. Then 425 x 30. Breaking down 30 into 3 x 10, so 425 x 10 is 4,250. Then 4,250 x 3. 4,000 x 3 = 12,000; 250 x 3 = 750. 12,000 + 750 = 12,750. That's right. Then 425 x 1 = 425. Adding them all: 42,500 + 12,750. Let's add 42,500 + 12,000 first, which is 54,500. Then add 750: 54,500 + 750 = 55,250. Then add 425: 55,250 + 425. 55,250 + 400 = 55,650. Then 55,650 + 25 = 55,675. Okay, seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to cross-verify. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm to check.\n\nMultiplying 425 by 131. Let's set it up vertically:\n\n      425\n    x 131\n    ------\n      425  (425 x 1)\n     4250  (425 x 30, shifted one position to the left)\n    42500  (425 x 100, shifted two positions to the left)\n    ------\n      55,675\n\nWait, that's the same result. So that's a good sign. Let me check each step again.\n\nFirst, multiplying 425 by 1 gives 425. Then multiplying 425 by 30, which is 425 x 3 x 10. 425 x 3 is 1,275. Then 1,275 x 10 is 12,750. Then multiplying 425 by 100 is 42,500. Adding all together: 425 + 12,750 + 42,500. Let's add 425 + 12,750 first. 425 + 12,750. 12,750 + 400 is 13,150. Then 13,150 + 25 is 13,175. Then 13,175 + 42,500. 13,175 + 40,000 = 53,175. Then 53,175 + 2,500 = 55,675. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use distributive property differently. 131 can be written as (100 + 30 + 1), which we already did. Alternatively, maybe break down 425 into 400 + 25. Let's try that.\n\n425 x 131 = (400 + 25) x 131 = 400x131 + 25x131.\n\nCalculating 400x131: 400 x 100 = 40,000; 400 x 30 = 12,000; 400 x 1 = 400. So 40,000 + 12,000 + 400 = 52,800.\n\nThen 25x131: 25 x 100 = 2,500; 25 x 30 = 750; 25 x 1 = 25. So 2,500 + 750 + 25 = 3,275.\n\nAdding 52,800 + 3,275. 52,800 + 3,000 = 55,800. Then +275 = 56,075. Wait, that's different. Wait, hold on. Wait, 25x131 is 3,275? Let me check that again. 25 x 131. 25 x 100 is 2,500. 25 x 30 is 750. 25 x 1 is 25. So 2,500 + 750 is 3,250. Then +25 is 3,275. Yes, that's correct. Then 52,800 + 3,275. 52,800 + 3,000 is 55,800. Then +275 is 56,075. Wait, but this contradicts the previous result. Wait, so now I have a problem. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, this is confusing. So when I broke down 131 into 100 + 30 + 1 and multiplied each by 425, I got 55,675. But when I broke down 425 into 400 + 25 and multiplied each by 131, I got 56,075. These two results are different. That means I must have made a mistake somewhere. Let me check both methods again.\n\nFirst method: 425 x 131.\n\nFirst approach: 425 x 100 = 42,500; 425 x 30 = 12,750; 425 x 1 = 425. Total: 42,500 + 12,750 = 55,250; 55,250 + 425 = 55,675.\n\nSecond method: 425 x 131 = (400 + 25) x 131 = 400x131 + 25x131.\n\n400x131: 400x100=40,000; 400x30=12,000; 400x1=400. Total: 40,000 + 12,000 = 52,000; 52,000 + 400 = 52,400. Wait, hold on, earlier I said 400x131 is 52,800. Wait, that's the mistake! 400x131 is 400 multiplied by 131. Let me compute that again. 400 x 131. 131 x 400. 131 x 4 is 524, so 524 x 100 = 52,400. So 400x131 is 52,400. Then 25x131 is 3,275. So 52,400 + 3,275 = 55,675. Ah, there we go. So my initial calculation in the second method was wrong. I must have miscalculated 400x131. I thought it was 52,800, but it's actually 52,400. So that's where the error came in. So correcting that, the second method also gives 55,675. So both methods agree. Phew, that was a bit of a brain twister. So important to check each step carefully.\n\nTherefore, the product of 425 and 131 is 55,675. Let me just confirm with another method. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm again.\n\nMultiplying 425 by 131:\n\n      425\n    x 131\n    ------\n      425  (425 x 1)\n     4250  (425 x 30, shifted one)\n    42500  (425 x 100, shifted two)\n    ------\n      55,675\n\nYes, adding those up: 425 + 4,250 + 42,500. Wait, hold on, in the standard multiplication, the second line is 4250, which is 425 x 30, shifted one position. So that's 4250. Then the third line is 42,500. So adding 425 + 4,250 + 42,500. Let's do that. 425 + 4,250 = 4,675. Then 4,675 + 42,500 = 47,175. Wait, that's not matching. Wait, no, that's not how the addition works. Wait, in the standard multiplication, the partial products are:\n\nFirst partial product: 425 (425 x 1)\n\nSecond partial product: 4250 (425 x 30, shifted one position)\n\nThird partial product: 42500 (425 x 100, shifted two positions)\n\nSo when you add them together, you have:\n\n425\n\n+4250\n\n+42500\n\nWhich is 425 + 4,250 = 4,675; 4,675 + 42,500 = 47,175. Wait, but that's not matching the total of 55,675. Wait, that can't be. Wait, no, that's not correct. Wait, no, the standard multiplication algorithm is different. Wait, no, actually, in the standard multiplication, the second partial product is shifted one position to the left, so it's 4250, which is 425 x 30, which is 12,750. Then the third partial product is 42,500, which is 425 x 100. So when you add them, you have 425 + 12,750 + 42,500. Which is 425 + 12,750 = 13,175; 13,175 + 42,500 = 55,675. So that's correct. So the confusion was because I misaligned the numbers when adding. So the partial products are 425, 12,750, and 42,500. So adding them gives 55,675. So that's correct.\n\nTherefore, all methods lead to the same result: 55,675. So I can be confident that the product of 425 and 131 is 55,675.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 425 and 131 is \\boxed{55675}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 425 and 131, we can break down 131 into 100, 30, and 1, and then multiply each part by 425 and sum the results:\n\n1. Calculate \\( 425 \\times 100 \\):\n   \\[\n   425 \\times 100 = 42500\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\( 425 \\times 30 \\):\n   \\[\n   425 \\times 30 = 12750\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\( 425 \\times 1 \\):\n   \\[\n   425 \\times 1 = 425\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the results together:\n   \\[\n   42500 + 12750 + 425\n   \\]\n\n5. First, add \\( 42500 \\) and \\( 12750 \\):\n   \\[\n   42500 + 12750 = 55250\n   \\]\n\n6. Then, add the remaining 425:\n   \\[\n   55250 + 425 = 55675\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 425 and 131 is \\(\\boxed{55675}\\).",
