\n\n132,600 - 200 = 132,400. \n\n132,400 - 4 = 132,396. \n\nBut according to the standard multiplication, it's 68,676. So there's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct? \n\nWait, maybe I messed up the standard multiplication. Let me check again. \n\nMultiplying 708 by 187. \n\nLet me write it out step by step:\n\nFirst, multiply 708 by 7 (units digit):\n\n708\nx   7\n-----\n4,956\n\nThen multiply 708 by 8 (tens digit):\n\n   708\nx  8\n-----\n5,664\nBut since it's the tens place, we need to multiply by 10, so 5,664 * 10 = 56,640\n\nThen multiply 708 by 1 (hundreds digit):\n\n   708\nx  1\n-----\n7,080\nBut since it's the hundreds place, multiply by 100, so 7,080 * 100 = 708,000\n\nNow add them all together:\n\n4,956 (units)\n56,640 (tens place multiplied by 10)\n708,000 (hundreds place multiplied by 100)\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, when you multiply by the hundreds digit, you have to multiply by 100, so the 708*1 = 708, then add two zeros: 70800. Wait, but in my previous calculation, I wrote 7,080, which is 708*10. Wait, no. Wait, confusion here. Let me clarify.\n\nWhen you multiply by the hundreds digit (the third digit from the right in 187), which is 1 in the hundreds place, you need to multiply the number by 100. So 708*1 = 708, then add two zeros: 70800. \n\nBut in my previous calculation, I wrote 7,080, which is 708*10. That's incorrect. So that's the mistake. \n\nSo correcting that:\n\nFirst part: 708*7 = 4,956\n\nSecond part: 708*8*10 = 5,664*10 = 56,640\n\nThird part: 708*1*100 = 708*100 = 70,800\n\nNow add them all together: 4,956 + 56,640 + 70,800\n\nLet's compute that:\n\n4,956 + 56,640 = 61,596\n\n61,596 + 70,800 = 132,396\n\nAh! Now that matches the first method. So earlier, I had a mistake in the standard multiplication where I forgot to add two zeros for the hundreds place. So the correct product is 132,396. \n\nBut wait, now I'm confused because two different methods gave me different results. Wait, no. Wait, the first method was 708*(200 - 13) = 141