      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 146 and 377. Hmm, let's see. I could just multiply them straight away, but that might be a bit tedious. Let me think if there's a smarter way to do this. Maybe breaking it down into smaller parts would make it easier. \n\nFirst, let me recall how multiplication works. 146 times 377. Let me write it out as 146 \u00d7 377. Maybe I can use the distributive property here. So, 146 is 100 + 40 + 6. So, if I distribute 377 over each of those parts, it would be 100\u00d7377 + 40\u00d7377 + 6\u00d7377. That might be easier than multiplying the whole numbers at once.\n\nLet me calculate each part step by step. \n\nStarting with 100\u00d7377. Well, 100 times anything is just the number with two zeros added. So 377\u00d7100 is 37,700. Got that.\n\nNext, 40\u00d7377. Hmm, 40 is 4 times 10, so 377\u00d74 would be 1,508 (since 300\u00d74=1,200, 70\u00d74=280, 7\u00d74=28; adding those gives 1,200+280=1,480+28=1,508). Then multiply by 10 to get 40\u00d7377: 1,508\u00d710=15,080. Okay, that seems right.\n\nNow, 6\u00d7377. Let's do that. 6\u00d7300=1,800; 6\u00d770=420; 6\u00d77=42. Adding those together: 1,800+420=2,220+42=2,262. So 6\u00d7377 is 2,262.\n\nNow, add all those three results together: 37,700 + 15,080 + 2,262. Let's do that step by step.\n\nFirst, 37,700 + 15,080. Let's add the thousands: 37,000 + 15,000 = 52,000. Then the hundreds: 700 + 800 = 1,500. Then the tens and units: 00 + 80 = 80. So adding those together: 52,000 + 1,500 = 53,500 + 80 = 53,580. Wait, that doesn't seem right. Wait, 37,700 + 15,080. Let me check again.\n\n37,700 + 15,080. Let's break it down:\n\n37,700 + 10,000 = 47,700\n\n47,700 + 5,000 = 52,700\n\n52,700 + 800 = 53,500\n\n53,500 + 0 = 53,500? Wait, but 15,080 is 15,000 + 80. So 37,700 + 15,000 = 52,700, then +80 = 52,780. Oh, I messed up the previous calculation. So 37,700 + 15,080 is 52,780. Then add 2,262 to that.\n\n52,780 + 2,262. Let's do that. 52,780 + 2,000 = 54,780. Then +262 = 55,042. So total is 55,042. \n\nWait, let me verify that again. Maybe I should use another method. Let's check each step again.\n\nFirst, 100\u00d7377=37,700. Correct.\n\n40\u00d7377: 377\u00d74=1,508, then \u00d710=15,080. Correct.\n\n6\u00d7377=2,262. Correct.\n\nNow adding 37,700 + 15,080. Let's do 37,700 + 15,080:\n\n37,700 + 15,000 = 52,700\n\n52,700 + 80 = 52,780. Yes, that's right.\n\nThen 52,780 + 2,262:\n\n52,780 + 2,000 = 54,780\n\n54,780 + 262 = 55,042. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo the total product is 55,042. Let me check with another method to be sure. Maybe the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nMultiplying 146 by 377:\n\nFirst, write 146 \u00d7 377.\n\nLet me do it step by step:\n\nMultiply 146 by 7 (units place):\n\n6\u00d77=42, write down 2, carry over 4.\n\nNext, multiply 40 by 7: 40\u00d77=280 + 4 (carry) = 284. Write down 4, carry over 28.\n\nThen multiply 100 by 7: 700 + 28 = 728. Write down 728.\n\nSo the first partial product is 728.\n\nNow multiply 146 by 70 (tens place):\n\n6\u00d770=420, write down 20, carry over 4.\n\n40\u00d770=2,800 + 4 = 2,804. Write down 2804.\n\nWait, but actually, when multiplying by the tens place (70), it's 146 \u00d7 70. So:\n\n6\u00d770=420, write 20, carry over 4.\n\n40\u00d770=2,800 + 4 = 2,804. So write 804, carry over 2.\n\nThen 100\u00d770=7,000 + 2 = 7,002.\n\nSo the second partial product is 2804, but written as 804 with a carryover of 2, making 2804. Wait, maybe I need to track the carries more carefully.\n\nWait, perhaps breaking it down:\n\nMultiplying 146 by 377:\n\nFirst, multiply 146 by 7 (units digit):\n\n146 \u00d7 7 = 1,022. Write down 22, carry over 10.\n\nWait, no, that's not the right way. Wait, actually, when multiplying by the units digit first, you do:\n\n146 \u00d7 7 = 1,022. So the units digit of the product is 2, but you have to carry over the tens digit. So 1,022. So the first partial product is 722, but shifted appropriately? Wait, maybe I'm confusing the steps.\n\nAlternatively, 