you actually multiply by 3 and then shift one position. So 1,770 shifted one position left is 17,700. But in the standard multiplication, you write it as:\n\n   590\nx  330\n--------\n    0 (units place)\n  1,770 (tens place)\n 177,000 (hundreds place)\n-------\n\nThen adding them up:\n\n0\n+1,770\n+177,000\n--------\n194,700\n\nYes, that's the same result as before. So that seems correct. So the product is 194,700. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method. Let's see. 590 * 330. Maybe convert 590 into 600 - 10. Then use distributive property. So (600 - 10) * 330 = 600*330 - 10*330. Let's compute that.\n\n600*330. 600*300 is 180,000, and 600*30 is 18,000. So 180,000 + 18,000 = 198,000. Then subtract 10*330, which is 3,300. So 198,000 - 3,300 = 194,700. Again, same answer. So that's three different methods, all leading to 194,700. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator to check. But since I don't have a calculator here, but I can check with another multiplication approach. Let's see. 590 * 330. Let's think of 590 as 59*10 and 330 as 33*10. So 59*33*10*10. So first compute 59*33, then multiply by 100.\n\nCompute 59*33. 59*30 is 1,770, and 59*3 is 177. So 1,770 + 177 = 1,947. Then multiply by 100: 1,947*100 = 194,700. Yep, same result. So that's another way to do it. So all methods converge to 194,700. \n\nTherefore, I think the product of 590 and 330 is 194,700. I can't see any mistakes in my reasoning, and multiple methods confirm the same answer. So I'm pretty confident that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 590 and 330 is \\boxed{194700}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 590 and 330, we can break down the multiplication into two parts and then add the results together:\n\n1. Calculate \\( 590 \\times 300 \\):\n   \\[\n   590 \\times 300 = 177000\n   \\]\n   This is because \\( 590 \\times 3 = 1770 \\), and multiplying by 100 gives \\( 1770 \\times 100 = 177000 \\).\n\n2. Calculate \\( 590 \\times 30 \\):\n   \\[\n   590 \\times 30 = 17700\n   \\]\n   This is because \\( 590 \\times 3 = 1770 \\), and multiplying by 10 gives \\( 1770 \\times 10 = 17700 \\).\n\n3. Add the results of the two calculations:\n   \\[\n   177000 + 17700 = 194700\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by converting the numbers into factors:\n   \\[\n   590 \\times 330 = (600 - 10) \\times 330 = 600 \\times 330 - 10 \\times 330\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   600 \\times 330 = 198000 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 10 \\times 330 = 3300\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   198000 - 3300 = 194700\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 590 and 330 is \\(\\boxed{194700}\\).",
