ous result. Wait, hold on. Wait, in the first method, I got 783,544, and in the second method, I got 7,745,944. That can't be right. There must be a mistake here.\n\nWait, no, actually, in the second method, when I multiplied 949 by the hundreds digit, which is 9, but in reality, the hundreds digit is 9, so the multiplier is 9,000. So, the partial product should be 9 \u00d7 856 \u00d7 1,000. Wait, but 9 \u00d7 856 is 7,704, so 7,704 \u00d7 1,000 is 7,704,000. Then, adding that to the previous partial products.\n\nBut in the first method, when I broke down 949 into 900 + 40 + 9, and multiplied each by 856, then added, I got 783,544. But in the second method, using the standard multiplication algorithm, I get 7,745,944. These numbers are way off. There's a mistake here. Let me check where I went wrong.\n\nWait, in the first method, I split 949 into 900 + 40 + 9. Then multiplied each by 856 and added. Let me check that again.\n\nFirst term: 856 \u00d7 900. 856 \u00d7 9 \u00d7 100. 856 \u00d7 9. Let's compute 856 \u00d7 9:\n\n800 \u00d7 9 = 7,200\n\n50 \u00d7 9 = 450\n\n6 \u00d7 9 = 54\n\nAdding those: 7,200 + 450 = 7,650 + 54 = 7,704. Then, 7,704 \u00d7 100 = 770,400.\n\nSecond term: 856 \u00d7 40. 856 \u00d7 4 \u00d7 10. 856 \u00d7 4: 800 \u00d7 4 = 3,200; 50 \u00d7 4 = 200; 6 \u00d7 4 = 24. So, 3,200 + 200 = 3,400 + 24 = 3,424. Then, \u00d710 gives 34,240.\n\nThird term: 856 \u00d7 9. As before, 7,704.\n\nAdding all three: 770,400 + 34,240 + 7,704.\n\n770,400 + 34,240 = 804,640. Then, 804,640 + 7,704 = 812,344. Wait, that's different from my previous calculation. Wait, so earlier I thought 800*900 was 720,000, but actually, 856*900 is 856*9*100=7,704*100=770,400. Oh! I made a mistake here. In my first method, I incorrectly calculated 800*900 as 720,000, but actually, it's 856*900. So, that's where the error came in.\n\nWait, so in the first method, I split 856 into 800 + 50 + 6, but when multiplying by 900, it's actually (800 + 50 + 6) * 900. So, 800*900 + 50*900 + 6