e, maybe do a manual check.\n\nWait, let's check 669 \u00d7 757.\n\nLet me write it out vertically:\n\n      669\n    \u00d7 757\n    ------\n      669 \u00d7 7 = 4,683\n      669 \u00d7 50 = 33,450\n      669 \u00d7 700 = 468,300\n    ------\n      ?\n\nAdding those three results:\n\n4,683\n33,450\n468,300\n--------\n506,433\n\nYes, that's the same as before. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe check with another multiplication. Let's take 669 \u00d7 757.\n\nAlternatively, break 757 into 700 + 50 + 7, which we already did. So that's the same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the fact that 757 = 700 + 57, so 669 \u00d7 700 + 669 \u00d7 57. Wait, 669 \u00d7 700 is 468,300. Then 669 \u00d7 57. Let's compute that.\n\n669 \u00d7 50 = 33,450\n\n669 \u00d7 7 = 4,683\n\nSo 33,450 + 4,683 = 38,133\n\nThen total is 468,300 + 38,133 = 506,433. Same result.\n\nOkay, so all methods lead to 506,433. Therefore, I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 669 and 757 is \\boxed{506433}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 669 and 757, we can break down each number into smaller parts and use the distributive property.\n\nFirst, we express 669 and 757 in terms of their components:\n- 669 = 600 + 60 + 9\n- 757 = 700 + 50 + 7\n\nWe then multiply each term of 669 by each term of 757 and sum the results:\n\n1. Multiply 600 by each term in 757:\n   - \\(600 \\times 700 = 420,000\\)\n   - \\(600 \\times 50 = 30,000\\)\n   - \\(600 \\times 7 = 4,200\\)\n\n2. Multiply 60 by each term in 757:\n   - \\(60 \\times 700 = 42,000\\)\n   - \\(60 \\times 50 = 3,000\\)\n   - \\(60 \\times 7 = 420\\)\n\n3. Multiply 9 by each term in 757:\n   - \\(9 \\times 700 = 6,300\\)\n   - \\(9 \\times 50 = 450\\)\n   - \\(9 \\times 7 = 63\\)\n\nNext, we add all these results together:\n\\[\n420,000 + 30,000 + 4,200 + 42,000 + 3,000 + 420 + 6,300 + 450 + 63\n\\]\n\nAdding step by step:\n\\[\n420,000 + 30,000 = 450,000\n\\]\n\\[\n450,000 + 4,200 = 454,200\n\\]\n\\[\n454,200 +